    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16145": {
      "title": "Department Chair and Tenure-Track, Mathematics Education (Open Rank)",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | Department Chair and Tenure-Track, Mathematics Education (Open Rank) Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Department Chair and Tenure-Track, Mathematics Education (Open Rank) Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Posting Number F000495 Position Title Department Chair and Tenure-Track, Mathematics Education (Open Rank) Department Mathematics and Actuarial Science Division Academic Affairs Faculty Status Full-time Faculty Supervisor Title Dean, College of Arts & Sciences Status Full-time Tenure-Track Faculty Tenure Track Yes Salary Range $72,000 to $88,000 Position Summary The Department of Mathematics and Actuarial Science in the College of Arts and Sciences at North Central College invites applications for a tenure-track position in mathematics education, to begin August 1, 2027. The duties in this role include: Provide instruction for a variety of mathematics and mathematics education courses, including elementary and secondary methods courses, carrying a teaching load equivalent to 20 credits per academic year. Mentor undergraduate students through one-on-one and group interactions and independent study opportunities. Pursue research activities in mathematics education, including new research projects, writing/dissemination of research, and presentation at conferences. Engage students in research when possible. As Coordinator of Mathematics Education, provide oversight for all mathematics education courses in keeping content aligned with NCTM and MAA standards and build connections with the mathematics education community in the local area. As department chair, provide leadership to the unit, including overseeing curriculum, staffing courses, evaluating and mentoring faculty, advising the Dean, and serving as liaison to other campus offices. Basic Qualifications: Basic qualifications are required at the time of application. The successful candidate will have completed a PhD in mathematics or mathematics education, or an EdD in mathematics education and an MS/MA in mathematics. PK-12 Educator License Preferred (current or expired) Applicants must demonstrate a commitment to excellence in teaching and mentoring of students, and ongoing scholarship. Applicants must demonstrate effective leadership in managing a department. Salary Range: The budgeted salary range for this position is $ 72,000 to $ 88,000 . About NCC : Founded in 1861, North Central College is a nationally recognized institution of about 3000 students, located in Naperville, IL, a thriving suburb just west of Chicago. The College is in a period of growth with the addition of a $60 million Science Center (2017), and a new Health Sciences and Engineering building (2020). North Central College is within walking distance of the Naperville Metra station and is next to downtown Naperville. The Naperville area is home to many excellent school districts and Naperville, IL has been ranked as one of the best places to live by Money magazine. North Central College values diversity, equity and inclusion and strongly encourages candidates who would enrich the diversity of the campus community to apply. Experience working with diverse student populations and communities is highly desirable. Application Window: Open date: August 15, 2026 Initial review date: October 1, 2026 Apply by initial review date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: October 15, 2026 Applications will continue to be accepted until this date, but those received after the review date will only be considered if the position has not yet been filled. For more information, please visit: https://www.northcentralcollege.edu/academic-affairs/prospective-and-incoming faculty Interested candidates should complete and submit an application with all required documentation online at: https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16011 Letters of recommendation will be requested automatically as soon as you submit your application. For further information, contact Matthew Pons, Chair MATH , mapons@noctrl.edu. Employment Conditions An offer of employment is contingent upon successful completion of the College’s background screening process. Number of Vacancies 1 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to increasing the diversity of our workforce. North Central College provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation, please notify the Office of Human Resource. Determinations on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Additional Information The budgeted salary range for this position is $72,000 to $88,000. The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the selected candidate’s experience and qualifications, including equivalent years in rank, training, and field or discipline; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. North Central College provides a comprehensive benefits package, which includes: All-inclusive medical, dental and vision group insurance Health and dependent care savings plans Employer-paid life and AD&D insurance 403(b) retirement plan with employer contributions Employee assistance plan resources Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you authorized to work in the US? YES NO * Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for work authorization? YES NO Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter/Letter of Interest Teaching Philosophy Statement of research interests and plans Resume or Curriculum Vitae Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16144": {
      "title": "General Maintenance/HVAC-Assistant Laborer",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | General Maintenance/HVAC-Assistant Laborer Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help General Maintenance/HVAC-Assistant Laborer Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Posting Number S000990 Position Title General Maintenance/HVAC-Assistant Laborer Department Physical Plant Administration Division Facilities Planning and Operations Supervisor Title Director of Physical Plant Status Full Time Regular Salary Range $25.00/Hour Position Summary To ensure that assigned buildings, including furnishings and mechanical and electrical systems therein, are functional on a continual basis. Description of Key Responsibilities Inspect the exterior and appropriate interior areas of assigned buildings on a regular basis. Identify, prioritize and perform or report necessary maintenance activities. Promptly report safety and security concerns. Maintain regular contact with sources of work assignments (i.e., supervisor, RT Tracker or assignment board) so that necessary activities are carried out in a timely fashion. Promptly report on completion of activities. Knowledge and ability to perform preventative maintenance, adjust, repair, and assist with the replacement of the following items: B&G pumps, hot water/steam boilers and associated controls, various hot water heaters and controls, A.C. and refrigeration units including: fan coil units, air handlers-belts, pulleys and drives, exhaust and intake fans, sump pumps, condensation pumps and lift station pumps, all type electric motors, belt/air filter changes, roof top units, various building controls, single and three phase electric, electric voltage’s ranging from 24V to 480 Volt A.C., electrical switch gear, motor starters and contactors. Maintain building access/egress areas – i.e., sidewalks, stairways – in a serviceable condition by removing debris and snow and ice. Carry out projects, which involve installations, renovations, remodeling, etc., as directed by supervisor, either individually or as part of a group. Coordinate all activities with building users and contractors (i.e., food service, cleaning services) so as to maximize efficiency and minimize disruption to building activities. May be assigned to temporarily work at other locations to substitute for personnel not available or to supplement personnel during times of unusually heavy workload. May be assigned to act in place of immediate supervisor during his/her absence, including the assumption of responsibility for that person’s duties and tasks. Perform other job-related duties and tasks as appropriately assigned. OTHER KEY RESPONSIBILITIES Support the College’s mission, vision and values, and contribute to the advancement of the College’s Strategic Plan. Demonstrate a strong commitment to continuous quality improvement. Demonstrate civil and inclusive behavior when interacting with staff, faculty, students and visitors to the College. Promote a flexible, collaborative and inclusive work and living environment and engage in educational opportunities to increase awareness and understanding of diversity and inclusion. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Ability to communicate in the English language, both orally and in writing, in order to receive assignments and instructions, understand written procedures and cautions, prepare written requests, record work completed, etc. Ability to utilize basic mathematical skills necessary for calculating quantities, preparing estimates, etc. Ability to accurately interpret drawings and schematics representing electrical, mechanical, architectural and structural systems. Ability to safely and effectively transport (which may involve lifting up to 50 pounds) and utilize materials and equipment used in facility servicing and maintenance and to work safely from ladders, scaffolds, and other elevated positions. Ability to perform a variety of building maintenance tasks requiring knowledge of and skills in working with electrical and mechanical systems (including HVAC ), plumbing, carpentry, masonry and painting projects. Ability to, as necessary, work outdoors in inclement weather and to work other-than-normal and, at times, long hours. Ability to carry out work efficiently and in a manner which will minimize interference with building operations. Ability to effectively communicate with, and relate positively to, a variety of people representing various building users. Ability to learn about, and work so as to contribute positively to, the College’s organization, operation, and philosophy/culture. Distinguishing Characteristics The condition of the College’s buildings has a profound effect on the overall appearance of the campus, as well as on the daily lives of all who live, study and work there. Perhaps most striking is the impact this function has on the quality of life of students, particularly those who reside on campus. The College’s work force which is responsible for buildings and grounds maintenance is generally made up of individuals who each perform a wide variety of duties, and whose work strongly affects a diverse group of users of the facilities. Thus, employees in this area are expected to be multi-skilled, flexible, and able to work cooperatively with each other and with other members of the campus community, often without direct supervision. Because of the nature of the work, reliable and regular attendance is essential. Because individuals in this position must interact regularly with a wide variety of members of the campus community, as well as, on occasion, inspectors from City and State agencies and the College’s insurance carrier, uniforms, furnished by the College, must be worn at times determined by the position supervisor. Individuals in this position may be required to work at times that are other than, or in addition to, normalhours and days, including responding at any time to severe weather and other emergency conditions.Also, individuals in this position can expect to participate in seasonal boiler-checks and be “on-call” duringnights & weekends for specified periods (presently one week at a time, approx. every fifth week);individuals must be available by telephone during these times. Education/Training A high school diploma or equivalent is required. Experience A minimum of one year of experience in building maintenance is required. (Specifically, HVAC ). Licenses or Certifications A valid driver’s license and acceptable driving record are required. EPA universal Refrigerant Certification is required. Is This a Temporary Position? No Employment Conditions An offer of employment is contingent upon successful completion of the College’s background screening process. Number of Vacancies 1 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to increasing the diversity of our workforce. NCC provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process, please notify the Office of Human Resources. Determinations for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Additional Information The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the candidate’s experience, qualifications, and training. North Central College provides a comprehensive benefits package, which includes: All-inclusive medical, dental and vision group insurance Health and dependent care savings plans Employer-paid life, AD&D and disability group insurance 403(b) retirement plan with employer contributions Employee assistance plan resources Tuition waiver for full-time employees, spouse and dependent children Generous paid time off plans, including 3 weeks vacation to start 15 paid holidays per year, including 5 days during NCC Winter Break Free access to campus health center, fitness and athletic facilities, and library Employee discount program Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you authorized to work in the US? YES NO * Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for work authorization? YES NO Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Interest EPA Universal Certification Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16139": {
      "title": "Instructional Designer",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | Instructional Designer Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Instructional Designer Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Posting Number S000984 Position Title Instructional Designer Department CAFE-Center for Advancement of Faculty Excellence Division Academic Affairs Supervisor Title Assistant Provost for Faculty Development and Innovation and Director, CAFE Status Full Time Regular Salary Range $56,000 - $62,000 Position Summary The Instructional Designer, housed in the Center for the Advancement of Faculty Excellence ( CAFE ), partners with faculty, academic programs, and campus colleagues to design, develop, evaluate, and improve high-quality learning experiences across in-person, hybrid, and online modalities. A central focus of this position is strengthening alignment among program learning outcomes, course learning outcomes, learning activities, assessments, and evidence of student learning. The Instructional Designer will help faculty and programs build and deploy in-person, hybrid, and online courses focused on learning outcomes or standards, rubrics, and aligned assessments within the College’s learning management system, and will support scalable approaches for collecting and using program-level evidence of student learning. The position also supports course quality, accessibility, faculty professional development, instructional technologies, and thoughtful use of emerging technologies. Experience with Canvas is strongly preferred, but candidates with substantial experience in another enterprise learning management system and a demonstrated ability to learn new platforms are encouraged to apply. Description of Key Responsibilities Partner with faculty and subject-matter experts to design, develop, revise, and evaluate courses and learning experiences using evidence-informed instructional design practices. Apply backward design and constructive alignment to connect program learning outcomes, course learning outcomes, instructional activities, assignments, assessments, and evidence of student learning. Build and configure course components within the learning management system, including modules, assignments, assessments, rubrics, learning outcomes or standards, and other instructional resources. Support academic programs in creating, organizing, and deploying learning-outcome or standards structures within the LMS , including mapping program learning outcomes to course outcomes and aligned assessments. Collaborate with faculty, academic leaders, and assessment colleagues to develop sustainable workflows for collecting, reviewing, and using evidence of program learning outcomes across courses and programs. Assist faculty with the design and use of rubrics, authentic assessments, and other measures that produce meaningful evidence of learner achievement. Develop and maintain course templates, blueprints, examples, guides, and other resources that promote clear, consistent, accessible, and learner-centered course design while preserving appropriate disciplinary flexibility. Review courses and digital learning materials for instructional quality, usability, accessibility, alignment, learner engagement, and effective use of instructional technology, and recommend practical improvements. Design and facilitate faculty workshops, consultations, institutes, and other professional learning related to course design, assessment, accessibility, online and hybrid teaching, Canvas or other LMS tools, and emerging instructional practices. Support the thoughtful and ethical use of generative artificial intelligence and other emerging technologies in course development, teaching, learning, and assessment. Evaluate, pilot, document, and support instructional technologies in collaboration with CAFE colleagues, Academic Affairs, Information Technology Services, Institutional Effectiveness, and other campus partners. Manage multiple instructional design projects simultaneously, establish clear timelines and deliverables, communicate progress and risks, and maintain accurate project documentation. Create clear faculty-facing documentation, tutorials, examples, and support resources for instructional design processes and learning technologies. Other duties as appropriately assigned. OTHER KEY RESPONSIBILITIES Support the College’s mission, vision and values, and contribute to the advancement of the College’s Strategic Plan. Demonstrate a strong commitment to continuous quality improvement. Demonstrate civil and inclusive behavior when interacting with staff, faculty, students and visitors to the College. Promote a flexible, collaborative and inclusive work and living environment and engage in educational opportunities to increase awareness and understanding of diversity and inclusion. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Demonstrated knowledge of instructional design, learning sciences, adult learning, backward design, and principles of effective teaching and learning. Demonstrated proficiency with learning outcomes, competencies or standards, curriculum alignment, assessment design, and rubrics. Ability to translate program-level learning goals into course-level outcomes, learning activities, assessments, and evidence that can be collected and interpreted. Experience using a learning management system to build or support courses. Canvas experience is strongly preferred, but equivalent LMS experience is acceptable. Knowledge of accessible and inclusive digital learning practices, including Universal Design for Learning and current accessibility expectations for course materials and technologies. Strong consultation and relationship-building skills, including the ability to work collaboratively with faculty who bring different levels of experience, confidence, and comfort with instructional technology. Excellent written, verbal, listening, facilitation, and presentation skills. Strong project-management, organizational, and time-management skills, including the ability to manage multiple projects and competing priorities with consistent follow-through. Ability to work independently, exercise sound judgment, solve problems, and contribute effectively within a collaborative team environment. Ability to learn new technologies and translate technical or pedagogical concepts into clear, practical guidance for faculty. Demonstrated commitment to accessible, inclusive, and learner-centered educational practices. Distinguishing Characteristics This faculty-facing, consultative instructional design role combines course design expertise with a strong emphasis on learning outcomes and assessment. The successful candidate must be able to move comfortably between one-on-one faculty consultation and institutionally scalable work involving LMS structures, program learning outcomes, course templates, rubrics, quality standards, and faculty-development resources. The position requires a collaborative, service-oriented professional who can build trust, ask productive questions, influence without formal authority, and translate instructional design and assessment principles into practical solutions. A systems-oriented perspective is especially valuable because the role connects programs, courses, outcomes, assessments, learning technologies, and continuous improvement. Education/Training Master’s degree in instructional design, instructional technology, curriculum and instruction, education, learning sciences, educational psychology, or a related field is preferred. A bachelor’s degree with a combination of relevant education, professional experience, and demonstrated expertise may be considered consistent with NCC requirements. Experience Two or more years of professional experience in instructional design, course design, faculty development, educational technology, e-learning, curriculum development, or a closely related area is preferred. Higher education experience is strongly preferred. Candidates should demonstrate experience building or substantially revising courses in a learning management system and applying learning outcomes, assessment, and alignment principles in authentic educational settings. Experience with one or more of the following is especially desirable: Canvas; LMS outcomes or standards functionality; program-level assessment; course templates or blueprints; online or hybrid course design; accessibility review; course-quality review; faculty professional development; HTML / CSS or digital content development; and educational uses of generative AI. Licenses or Certifications Valid Illinois driver’s license and acceptable driving record are required. No other license or certification is required. Relevant certifications or professional development in Canvas, Quality Matters, OSCQR , accessibility, instructional design, online learning, or related areas are valued. Is This a Temporary Position? No Employment Conditions An offer of employment is contingent upon successful completion of the College’s background screening process. Number of Vacancies 1 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to increasing the diversity of our workforce. NCC provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process, please notify the Office of Human Resources. Determinations for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Additional Information The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the candidate’s experience, qualifications, and training. North Central College provides a comprehensive benefits package, which includes: All-inclusive medical, dental and vision group insurance Health and dependent care savings plans Employer-paid life, AD&D and disability group insurance 403(b) retirement plan with employer contributions Employee assistance plan resources Tuition waiver for full-time employees, spouse and dependent children Generous paid time off plans, including 3 weeks vacation to start 15 paid holidays per year, including 5 days during NCC Winter Break Free access to campus health center, fitness and athletic facilities, and library Employee discount program Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you authorized to work in the US? YES NO * Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for work authorization? YES NO Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Interest Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16134": {
      "title": "Windows End User Support Specialist",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | Windows End User Support Specialist Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Windows End User Support Specialist Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Posting Number S000989 Position Title Windows End User Support Specialist Department Information Technology Services Division Information & Technology Supervisor Title Telecom/Comp Support Operations Manager Status Full Time Regular Salary Range $60,000 - $65,000 Position Summary To provide service and support for Windows desktops, laptops, and peripherals attached to the NCC campus-wide network. The person in this position works with faculty, staff, and students in academic and administrative departments. This will include sourcing, testing and debugging, implementing, servicing, integrating new and existing systems, and learning new technologies quickly. Ensures the implementation of best practice methodologies. Description of Key Responsibilities Responsible for support of college owned Windows workstations plus peripherals for faculty, staff, student labs, teaching stations and residence halls. This support covers hardware, software and all network client functions. Once an open Help Desk call is logged, the necessary steps will be taken to troubleshoot, see the call through to satisfactory resolution and then complete documentation to close the call. When needed, will work with network administrators, Ellucian administrators and telecommunications administrators to solve reported problems. The candidate would also work to update the Help Desk staff for information that could be used for future knowledge base / training / troubleshooting opportunities. Maintains college standard software with the latest firmware and software patches on all college owned Windows computers, and other applicable peripherals. Responsible for insuring that appropriate/requested software is loaded in labs and classrooms for instructional purposes. Responsible for support of the software and hardware of printers, managed print services and multi-function print/copy/scan devices on campus. Plan, setup, upgrade and configure new Windows desktops, laptops, and peripherals. This will often include staff and faculty office moves across campus. The candidate would be responsible for moving and/or coordinating the move of computing related equipment during these activities. Responsible for improving the organization of equipment and peripherals as well as finding more efficient and effective methods to deliver service. Function as a backup resource to our Apple End User Support Specialist and our Telecommunications functions. Requisite on the job training for these activities would be provided if the candidate does not already possess these skillsets. Demonstrate civil and inclusive behavior when interacting with staff, faculty, students, and visitors to the College. Promote a flexible, collaborative, and inclusive work and living environment and engage in educational opportunities to increase awareness and understanding of diversity and inclusion. Fulfill other related duties as assigned by designated supervisor(s). Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities An individual in this position must possess the proficiency, wherewithal and knowledge of principles and processes for providing high-quality service to both internal and external constituents of the College. Ability to communicate technical ideas to business users and other teams (design, Q.A.). Willingness to pursue additional training and to share that knowledge to enhance the functionality and resources of the infrastructure technology team. Ability to learn new technologies quickly Ability to work on multiple projects with overlapping critical tasks. Ability to document software/hardware projects clearly and concisely. Ability to consistently demonstrate excellent interpersonal and communication skills in dealing with persons both internal and external to the College, in person, on the telephone and electronically. Ability to work effectively as a member of a staff where cooperation and teamwork are essential. Ability to work both as a team member and independently, depending on the nature of the task, and provide excellent customer service skills. Strong experience with computer repairs and installation of computers. Experience effectively using a ticket tracking system. Strong knowledge with Windows, Linux and the Microsoft Office Suite. While not required, some experience with Apple MAC Operating Systems and Apple related software is a plus. Experience effectively using endpoint management systems such as, but not limited, to Zenworks, SCCM , or Intune is preferred. Strong knowledge in supporting mobile devices and tablets (aka companion devices) on both the Apple and Android platforms. Ability to deal with several tasks and work with a large number of individuals concurrently; requiring a person who is well organized, detail oriented, flexible and able to effectively prioritize tasks. Experience delivering presentations and conducting training and workshops. Ability and willingness to pursue additional training and to share such new knowledge to enhance the functionality and resources of the ITS team. Distinguishing Characteristics As this position includes a high degree of interaction with faculty, staff and students, the position requires a detail-oriented and results-driven individual who works well within team environments and has excellent communication skills, as well as good client interaction skills. Education/Training Bachelor’s degree in Computer Science, Interactive Media Studies, or a related field. A+ and Network+ certifications preferred. Experience A minimum of two years’ work experience in the Information Technology field required. Knowledge and experience in a College environment preferred. Licenses or Certifications Is This a Temporary Position? No Employment Conditions An offer of employment is contingent upon successful completion of the College’s background screening process. Number of Vacancies 1 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to increasing the diversity of our workforce. NCC provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process, please notify the Office of Human Resources. Determinations for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Additional Information The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the candidate’s experience, qualifications, and training. North Central College provides a comprehensive benefits package, which includes: All-inclusive medical, dental and vision group insurance Health and dependent care savings plans Employer-paid life, AD&D and disability group insurance 403(b) retirement plan with employer contributions Employee assistance plan resources Tuition waiver for full-time employees, spouse and dependent children Generous paid time off plans, including 3 weeks vacation to start 15 paid holidays per year, including 5 days during NCC Winter Break Free access to campus health center, fitness and athletic facilities, and library Employee discount program Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you authorized to work in the US? YES NO * Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for work authorization? YES NO Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Interest Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16129": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant to the VP for Student Life and Dean of Students",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | Administrative Assistant to the VP for Student Life and Dean of Students Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Administrative Assistant to the VP for Student Life and Dean of Students Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Posting Number S000988 Position Title Administrative Assistant to the VP for Student Life and Dean of Students Department Student Affairs Division Student Affairs Supervisor Title Vice President for Student Life & Dean of Students Status Full Time Regular Salary Range $24 - $25 per hour Position Summary The Administrative Assistant provides high-level, proactive administrative support to the Vice President for Student Life and Dean of Students. The position supports the effective operation of the Vice President’s office by managing complex scheduling and priorities, coordinating meetings and communications, preparing materials, tracking projects and follow-up items, and supporting key divisional and institutional activities. The Administrative Assistant serves as an important liaison between the Vice President’s office and students, families, faculty, staff, senior leadership, and external constituents. The position requires strong judgment, discretion, organization, initiative, and the ability to anticipate needs and manage multiple priorities in a fast-paced environment. Occasional early morning, evening, or weekend work may be required to support key institutional activities, Board meetings, major Student Life events, or emergency situations. Description of Key Responsibilities Provide proactive administrative support to the Vice President for Student Life and Dean of Students, anticipating needs and supporting the effective organization, coordination, and execution of priorities within the Vice President’s office. Manage the Vice President’s calendar, scheduling, and meeting preparation, coordinating complex priorities, preparing agendas and background materials, attending designated meetings to capture key information as appropriate, and tracking key decisions, commitments, and follow-up. Coordinate executive and divisional communications, including preparing routine correspondence, managing designated office email accounts and communication channels, coordinating communications, maintaining appropriate records, and supporting timely and consistent information flow. Support the preparation of executive and divisional reports, presentations, briefings, and other materials by coordinating information and data collection, organizing and synthesizing content, and preparing materials for the Vice President’s review and use. Support the coordination and tracking of divisional and institutional priorities and projects, maintaining appropriate documentation, timelines, and follow-up and helping provide the Vice President with clear administrative sightlines regarding progress and upcoming deadlines. Coordinate administrative support for executive, divisional, and institutional activities, including Board and Cabinet-related materials and follow-up, divisional leadership meetings, retreats, searches, onboarding, and other key activities involving the Vice President or division. Support the effective daily operation of the Vice President’s office, including coordination of front office services and the selection, training, scheduling, supervision, and coordination of student employees and other designated administrative support staff assigned to the office. Serve as a primary administrative point of contact and help ensure a professional, responsive, and welcoming experience for students, families, faculty, staff, and other constituents. Provide administrative and system support for Student Life processes managed through Maxient and other designated systems, helping to ensure effective workflow, accurate documentation, and appropriate coordination with responsible Student Life staff. Provide administrative support for divisional budget and financial processes, including maintaining and tracking financial information, preparing routine reports and documentation, and supporting the Vice President in monitoring divisional expenditures and budget activity. Maintain effective administrative systems and processes and handle confidential and sensitive information with a high degree of discretion, sound judgment, attention to detail, and appropriate adherence to College policies. Perform other related duties as appropriately assigned. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Excellent service is critical to the success of any organization that deals with customers. Strong customer service skills can build retention and visibility as NC strives to distinguish itself from its competitors. An individual in this position must possess the proficiency, wherewithal and knowledge of principles and processes for providing high-quality customer and personal services to both internal and external customers of the College. Demonstrated ability to provide proactive administrative support, anticipate needs, manage competing priorities, and work effectively with minimal supervision. Excellent organizational and time-management skills, with the ability to manage multiple activities, monitor progress, meet deadlines, and maintain strong attention to detail. Excellent written and verbal communication skills and the ability to draft, organize, synthesize, and professionally present information for executive review and use. Strong interpersonal and customer-service skills and the ability to interact professionally and sensitively with students, families, faculty, staff, senior leaders, trustees, and external constituents. Demonstrated judgment and discretion in handling confidential and sensitive student, personnel, financial, and institutional information. Ability to compile, organize, summarize, and report information and data accurately, concisely, and in a professionally formatted manner. Proficiency with contemporary office technology, administrative systems, and productivity tools, with the ability and willingness to learn new systems and identify opportunities to improve administrative processes. Ability to coordinate projects, meetings, communications, and follow-up across multiple individuals and areas while maintaining appropriate records and documentation. Ability to supervise, train, schedule, and provide direction and feedback to student employees and coordinate other designated administrative support staff assigned to the office. Ability to work collaboratively and effectively with individuals from a wide variety of backgrounds and contribute to a professional, welcoming, and service-oriented environment. Distinguishing Characteristics The Administrative Assistant serves as a trusted administrative partner to the Vice President for Student Life and Dean of Students and plays a central role in ensuring the effective organization and coordination of the Vice President’s office. The position requires the ability to anticipate administrative needs, manage competing priorities, maintain appropriate follow-through, and support the Vice President in staying prepared and informed. The Administrative Assistant regularly handles confidential and sensitive information and must exercise sound judgment and discretion. The individual must be highly organized, attentive to detail, comfortable working independently, and able to develop effective systems that support calendar management, meeting preparation, communication, project coordination, and divisional operations. Because the Vice President’s office frequently serves as a point of connection for students, families, faculty, staff, senior leaders, and other constituents, the Administrative Assistant must also demonstrate excellent interpersonal skills and a service-oriented approach. Education/Training Education/Training : Bachelor’s degree required. In lieu of a degree, a minimum of five years of progressively responsible executive-level administrative support experience may be considered. Experience Experience: Three years of experience supporting a senior executive in a fast-paced professional setting is preferred. Higher education experience is preferred but not required. Demonstrated experience managing complex schedules, projects, communications, confidential information, and administrative processes with minimal oversight is preferred. Licenses or Certifications Is This a Temporary Position? No Employment Conditions An offer of employment is contingent upon successful completion of the College’s background screening process. Number of Vacancies 1 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to increasing the diversity of our workforce. NCC provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process, please notify the Office of Human Resources. Determinations for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Additional Information The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the candidate’s experience, qualifications, and training. North Central College provides a comprehensive benefits package, which includes: All-inclusive medical, dental and vision group insurance Health and dependent care savings plans Employer-paid life, AD&D and disability group insurance 403(b) retirement plan with employer contributions Employee assistance plan resources Tuition waiver for full-time employees, spouse and dependent children Generous paid time off plans, including 3 weeks vacation to start 15 paid holidays per year, including 5 days during NCC Winter Break Free access to campus health center, fitness and athletic facilities, and library Employee discount program Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you authorized to work in the US? YES NO * Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for work authorization? YES NO Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Interest Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16115": {
      "title": "Adjunct Pool - Instructors in Biology",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | Adjunct Pool - Instructors in Biology Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Adjunct Pool - Instructors in Biology Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Posting Number F000465 Position Title Adjunct Pool - Instructors in Biology Department BIOLOGY Division Academic Affairs Faculty Status Per Course Part-time Faculty Supervisor Title Chair, Department of Biology Status Per Course Part-time Faculty Tenure Track No Salary Range $960-$1,040 per credit hour Position Summary The Department of Biology at North Central College invites applications for adjunct teaching opportunities on a per-course basis. Courses regularly available include: Introductory Cell Biology Lab 200-level Ecology Lab 200-level Genetics Lab Human Biology (lecture and lab for non-majors) Applicants are encouraged to indicate in their cover letter which course areas they are interested in (multiple selections are welcome). Candidates should hold at least a master’s degree in biology or a closely related field and have experience teaching at the college level. A terminal degree is preferred. Adjunct instructors are required to deliver course content consistent with learning outcomes, contribute to the creation of assignments and other assessments, evaluate and grade student work, and be available for office hours to support student success. These appointments are on a course-by-course basis. This posting is continuous to develop a qualified pool of interested candidates. If your academic area of interest/expertise meets our programmatic needs at any time, you may be contacted to discuss teaching opportunities. These needs change from semester to semester. Faculty Associate (adjunct) positions are hired on one semester, renewable contracts with loads that do not exceed 8 credit hours; one course per semester is the norm. The position is a non-benefits eligible, fixed-term appointment with no tenure eligibility. The number of courses is contingent upon unit needs, funding, and sufficient enrollment. Founded in 1861, North Central College is a nationally recognized institution of about 3000 students, located in Naperville, IL, a thriving suburb just west of Chicago. North Central College is within walking distance of the Naperville Metra station and is next to downtown Naperville. For more information, please visit: http://www.noctrl.edu . North Central College is an EEO Employer. Interested candidates should complete the application at: https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/15145 . You will be asked to submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and contact information for three professional references. For further information, contact Jennifer Sallee, Chair, Department of Biology, at jlsallee@noctrl.edu. The review of applications will occur as the College has needs for a particular course. Applications will continue to be accepted and reviewed on an ongoing basis. This pool recruitment will be live throughout the academic year, and the College may or may not hire from it, depending on course needs. A background check and official transcripts are required prior to employment. Employment Conditions An offer of employment is contingent upon successful completion of the College’s background screening process. Number of Vacancies Varies Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to increasing the diversity of our workforce. North Central College provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation, please notify the Office of Human Resource. Determinations on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Close Date Open Until Filled No Additional Information The budgeted salary range for this position is $960-$1,040 per credit hour. The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the selected candidate’s experience and qualifications, including equivalent years in rank, training, and field or discipline; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you authorized to work in the US? YES NO * Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for work authorization? YES NO Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter/Letter of Interest Optional Documents Contact Information for 3 Professional References North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16114": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Health Sciences",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | Lecturer in Health Sciences Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Lecturer in Health Sciences Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Posting Number F000501 Position Title Lecturer in Health Sciences Department School of Education and Health Sciences Division Academic Affairs Faculty Status Full-time Faculty Supervisor Title Program Director for Health Sciences Status Full-time Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Tenure Track No Salary Range $55,000 – $60,000 Position Summary The Department of Health Sciences in the School of Education and Health Sciences at North Central College invites applications for a full-time, non-tenure-track Lecturer position in Health Science, beginning in Fall 2027. This is a 9.5-month, full-time faculty appointment with a 24-credit annual teaching load. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate courses in the Health Science curriculum. Teaching assignments will be based on departmental needs as well as the candidate’s qualifications and interests and may include courses such as: Introduction to Health Sciences Community Health Epidemiology in the Health Sciences Evidence-Based Practice in Health Sciences Research Methods in the Health Sciences Cultural Competency and Health Literacy Health Systems and Informatics Leadership in Healthcare In addition to teaching, the successful candidate will serve as a core faculty member within the Health Science program. Responsibilities include advising and mentoring undergraduate students, recruiting and engaging prospective students, participating in departmental and institutional service, collaborating with faculty on program initiatives, and supporting the continued growth and success of the Health Science program. Candidates should demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in undergraduate teaching, student mentorship, and collaborative engagement within an academic community. Qualifications A doctoral or terminal degree in Health Science or a closely related discipline is preferred; however, ABD candidates and applicants with a master’s degree in Health Science or a related field and demonstrated success teaching at the college level are encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will demonstrate excellence in undergraduate teaching, effective student mentoring, and a commitment to fostering an inclusive and engaging learning environment. Salary Range: The budgeted salary range for this position is: $55,000 – $60,000. About NCC : North Central College values diversity, equity and inclusion and strongly encourages candidates who would enrich the diversity of the campus community to apply. Experience working with diverse student populations and communities is highly desirable. Founded in 1861, North Central College is a nationally recognized institution of about 3000 students, located in Naperville, IL, a thriving suburb just west of Chicago. The College is in a period of growth with the addition of a $60 million Science Center (2017), and a new Health Sciences and Engineering building (2020). North Central College is within walking distance of the Naperville Metra station and is next to downtown Naperville. The Naperville area is home to many excellent school districts and Naperville, IL has been ranked as one of the best places to live by Money magazine. North Central College is an EEO Employer. For more information, please visit: http://www.noctrl.edu Interested candidates should complete the application at: https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16112 Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. For additonal information , please contact Kristin Patel, Chair, Department of Health Sciences at kapaptel@noctrl.edu. Employment Conditions An offer of employment is contingent upon successful completion of the College’s background screening process. Number of Vacancies 1 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to increasing the diversity of our workforce. North Central College provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation, please notify the Office of Human Resource. Determinations on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Additional Information The budgeted salary range for this position is $55,000 – $60,000. The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the selected candidate’s experience and qualifications, including equivalent years in rank, training, and field or discipline; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. North Central College provides a comprehensive benefits package, which includes: All-inclusive medical, dental and vision group insurance Health and dependent care savings plans Employer-paid life and AD&D insurance 403(b) retirement plan with employer contributions Employee assistance plan resources Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you authorized to work in the US? YES NO * Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for work authorization? YES NO Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter/Letter of Interest Teaching Philosophy Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16111": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Faculty in Health Sciences and Program Director (Open Rank)",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | Tenure-Track Faculty in Health Sciences and Program Director (Open Rank) Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Tenure-Track Faculty in Health Sciences and Program Director (Open Rank) Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Posting Number F000500 Position Title Tenure-Track Faculty in Health Sciences and Program Director (Open Rank) Department School of Education and Health Sciences Division Academic Affairs Faculty Status Full-time Faculty Supervisor Title Chair, Department of Health Sciences Status Full-time Tenure-Track Faculty Tenure Track Yes Salary Range $65,000 – $70,000 Position Summary The Department of Health Sciences in the School of Education and Health Sciences at North Central College invites applications for a full-time, 9.5-month tenure-track faculty position in Health Science, to begin Fall 2027. This position includes a leadership appointment as Program Director of the undergraduate Health Science program. The undergraduate Health Science program is one of the College’s largest and fastest-growing academic programs, serving more than 200 undergraduate majors. The successful candidate will provide visionary leadership to support the continued growth, quality, and innovation of the program while contributing to excellence in teaching, scholarship, and service. As Program Director, the successful candidate will oversee the undergraduate Health Science program and collaborate closely with the department chair, college leadership, and external partners. Responsibilities may include, but are not limited to: Providing leadership for the undergraduate Health Science program. Leading curriculum development, review, and continuous program improvement. Coordinating program assessment as applicable. Advising and mentoring current and prospective Health Science students. Managing program planning, budgeting, and resource allocation. Developing the course schedule in collaboration with department chair and faculty. Planning and facilitating program events that foster student engagement, professional development, and community within the major. Building and maintaining relationships with healthcare organizations, community partners, and industry professionals to expand experiential learning opportunities. Collaborating with graduate programs to develop and strengthen accelerated pathways and graduate education opportunities for Health Science students. Supporting student recruitment, retention, and overall program visibility. This tenure-track position carries a 20-credit annual teaching load, with a portion of teaching responsibilities reassigned to support Program Director duties. Teaching assignments will be based on departmental needs and the successful candidate’s expertise and may include undergraduate courses such as: Introduction to Health Sciences Community Health Epidemiology in the Health Sciences Evidence-Based Practice Research Methods in the Health Sciences Cultural Competency and Health Literacy Health Systems and Informatics In addition to program leadership and teaching, tenure-track faculty are expected to maintain an active program of scholarship, mentor undergraduate students, and contribute to the intellectual life of the College through departmental, school, and institutional service. Successful candidates will demonstrate a commitment to excellence in teaching, scholarly engagement, collaborative leadership, and fostering an inclusive learning environment. Qualifications A doctoral degree (earned by the time of appointment) in Health Science or a closely related discipline is required. Candidates should demonstrate evidence of excellence in undergraduate teaching, a developing or established record of scholarship appropriate for a tenure-track appointment, and a commitment to student mentorship. Administrative or program leadership experience, curriculum development, assessment, and experience building partnerships with healthcare organizations or graduate programs are highly desirable. Experience working collaboratively across disciplines and contributing to innovative academic initiatives is also preferred. Salary Range: The budgeted salary range for this position is: $65,000 – $70,000 About NCC : North Central College values diversity, equity and inclusion and strongly encourages candidates who would enrich the diversity of the campus community to apply. Experience working with diverse student populations and communities is highly desirable. Founded in 1861, North Central College is a nationally recognized institution of about 3000 students, located in Naperville, IL, a thriving suburb just west of Chicago. The College is in a period of growth with the addition of a $60 million Science Center (2017), and a new Health Sciences and Engineering building (2020). North Central College is within walking distance of the Naperville Metra station and is next to downtown Naperville. The Naperville area is home to many excellent school districts and Naperville, IL has been ranked as one of the best places to live by Money magazine. North Central College is an EEO Employer. For more information, please visit: http://www.noctrl.edu Interested candidates should complete the application at: https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16105 Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. For additonal information, please contact Kristin Patel, Chair, Department of Health Sciences, at kapaptel@noctrl.edu Employment Conditions An offer of employment is contingent upon successful completion of the College’s background screening process. Number of Vacancies 1 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to increasing the diversity of our workforce. North Central College provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation, please notify the Office of Human Resource. Determinations on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Additional Information The budgeted salary range for this position is $65,000 – $70,000. The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the selected candidate’s experience and qualifications, including equivalent years in rank, training, and field or discipline; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. North Central College provides a comprehensive benefits package, which includes: All-inclusive medical, dental and vision group insurance Health and dependent care savings plans Employer-paid life and AD&D insurance 403(b) retirement plan with employer contributions Employee assistance plan resources Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you authorized to work in the US? YES NO * Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for work authorization? YES NO Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter/Letter of Interest Teaching Philosophy Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16104": {
      "title": "Coordinator for Civic and Cultural Engagement",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | Coordinator for Civic and Cultural Engagement Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Coordinator for Civic and Cultural Engagement Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Posting Number S000986 Position Title Coordinator for Civic and Cultural Engagement Department Academic Affairs Division Academic Affairs Supervisor Title Assistant Provost for Student Success and Engaged Learning Status Full Time Regular Salary Range $48,000 - $53,000 Position Summary The Office of Engaged Learning at North Central College is committed to the integration of experiential learning into the academic experience of every student. Reporting to the Assistant Provost for Student Success and Engaged Learning, the Coordinator for Civic and Cultural Engagement supports high-impact practices, including Community-Engaged Learning, the Center for Social Impact, and Cultural Events. The coordinator collaborates with faculty and staff, provides administrative and logistical support, maintains program resources, creates internal and external messaging, and assists with assessment and evaluation. The coordinator cultivates and sustains community partnerships while supporting faculty, staff, and students engaged in community-based learning and volunteerism. The coordinator manages partnerships to support NC students in internships with non-profits. This position is integral to cultivating an intellectual, civically-engaged community of learners at North Central College and requires a commitment to community engagement, an equitable democracy, and advancing the common good. Evening and weekend work may be required. Description of Key Responsibilities Support the Assistant Provost in the planning, implementation, and assessment of Community-Engaged Learning ( CEL ) and other engaged learning initiatives. Support faculty in integrating CEL into courses by providing guidance, resources, and partnership procedures. Develop and maintain community partnerships, serve as the primary contact for external non-profit organizations, and manage partnership records and resources. Expand community engagement and place-based learning opportunities, including offering NC students regular opportunities for volunteerism. Coordinate efforts to apply Federal Work-Study funds for work and internship opportunities in on- and off-campus partnerships. Chair the Hearst Make A Difference, Volunteers In Action ( VIA ), and John Bean scholarship committees, and supervise the Student Volunteer Coordinator. Coordinate the Washington Internship Institute program, including support for student advising, recruitment, and applications, as well as the management of Eimermann Washington Semester funds. Support the planning and execution of the College’s Cultural Events Series. Manage external partnerships, contracts, and hospitality for Cultural Events and the Center for Social Impact. Support the Faculty Director of the Center for Social Impact in planning and evaluating programming focused on civic engagement and action-based research. Develop communications, including social media, to promote engaged learning initiatives and community partnerships. Collaborate on assessment, data collection, and strategic initiatives related to high-impact educational practices. Foster a collaborative, inclusive, and respectful campus environment while supporting the College’s commitment to community engagement, an equitable democracy, and advancing the common good. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities An individual in this position must possess the proficiency, wherewithal and knowledge of principles and processes for providing high‐quality service to both internal and external constituents of the College. Ability to communicate, both verbally and in writing, with particular emphasis on the ability to produce documents that are attractively formatted and present proper spelling, grammar and punctuation. Ability to continuously demonstrate outstanding interpersonal skills in dealing effectively and pleasantly with students, faculty, administration, staff, community partners, and the general public, both on the telephone and in person. Ability to work cooperatively as a team member. Ability to be involved with several activities concurrently, to continually monitor their status, and to prioritize activities so that deadlines are met. Proficient with Microsoft Office Suite or related software. Adept at telling the story of experiential learning through social media, written documents, and public speaking. Distinguishing Characteristics The position requires interaction with faculty, students, staff, and community partners on a regular basis. It requires someone who can build and sustain relationships, take initiative, and execute projects independently. The individual must be flexible and work cooperatively and flexibly with others, assisting other staff members in the performance of their work. Strong interpersonal skills are essential in this role. Education/Training Bachelor’s degree required; Master’s preferred. Experience Prior experience in engaging with or working directly in non-profits strongly preferred. Higher education and familiarity with college student life issues, particularly around equity and diversity, is strongly preferred. Work experience in community relations, volunteer engagement, event planning, or related areas is preferred. Licenses or Certifications Is This a Temporary Position? No Employment Conditions An offer of employment is contingent upon successful completion of the College’s background screening process. Number of Vacancies 1 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to increasing the diversity of our workforce. NCC provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process, please notify the Office of Human Resources. Determinations for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Additional Information The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the candidate’s experience, qualifications, and training. North Central College provides a comprehensive benefits package, which includes: All-inclusive medical, dental and vision group insurance Health and dependent care savings plans Employer-paid life, AD&D and disability group insurance 403(b) retirement plan with employer contributions Employee assistance plan resources Tuition waiver for full-time employees, spouse and dependent children Generous paid time off plans, including 3 weeks vacation to start 15 paid holidays per year, including 5 days during NCC Winter Break Free access to campus health center, fitness and athletic facilities, and library Employee discount program Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you authorized to work in the US? YES NO * Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for work authorization? YES NO Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Interest Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16099": {
      "title": "Senior Director of Housing and Residential Education",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | Senior Director of Housing and Residential Education Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Senior Director of Housing and Residential Education Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Posting Number S000985 Position Title Senior Director of Housing and Residential Education Department Residence Life Division Student Affairs Supervisor Title Assistant Dean of Student Life Status Full Time Regular Salary Range $80,000-$90,000 Position Summary The Senior Director of Housing and Residential Education provides leadership for the College’s residential experience, including residential education, housing operations, residential conduct administration, crisis response, and student support initiatives. Reporting to the Assistant Dean for Student Life and Strategic Initiatives and serving as a member of the Student Life Leadership Team, the Senior Director collaborates with campus partners to foster safe, inclusive, and educationally purposeful communities that support learning, well-being, belonging, and persistence. Description of Key Responsibilities Provide leadership for Housing and Residential Education, including residential education, occupancy management, student engagement, leadership development, crisis response, and community development initiatives. Develop and implement a residential experience model grounded in student development theory, belonging, learning outcomes, assessment, and student support. Provide direct supervision and leadership for the Assistant Director for Residential Experience, Housing Coordinator, Residence Hall Directors, graduate assistants, and other assigned staff. Lead the recruitment, hiring, onboarding, training, supervision, evaluation, and professional growth of professional staff, graduate assistants, and student employees. Provide oversight for housing operations, occupancy management, room assignments, residential transitions, break housing, and summer housing operations in collaboration with the Housing Coordinator. Direct and participate in short- and long-range planning, including budget development and management, occupancy management, assessment, and initiatives that strengthen the residential experience and support divisional priorities. Collaborate with Enrollment Management and campus partners to support recruitment, retention, and student engagement initiatives. Provide leadership for residential conduct processes and support the consistent application of institutional policies and developmental responses that promote accountability and student learning. Adjudicate residential conduct matters and selected non-residential and off-campus conduct cases, as assigned. Review and resolve student conduct appeals in collaboration with the Assistant Director for Residential Experience and appropriate Student Life administrators. Partner with Assistant Deans regarding complex student cases, behavioral intervention efforts, conduct appeals, and implementation of institutional policies. Train and support professional staff serving as conduct officers and promote restorative and developmental approaches that foster accountability and learning. Serve as co-chair of the College’s CARE Team and provide collaborative leadership in case consultation, coordinated interventions, and resource referrals. Participate in crisis management, emergency response, and coordinated student support efforts. Serve as a member of the Student Life Leadership Team and contribute to divisional assessment initiatives, retention efforts, and strategic priorities. Represent Housing and Residential Education and Student Life at orientation programs, admissions events, family programs, and other campus initiatives. Assist with annual crime statistics, Clery reporting responsibilities, and related risk management initiatives. Develop and manage departmental budgets and contribute to planning efforts that support enrollment and retention goals. Develop, manage, and monitor departmental operating budgets and resources to ensure fiscal responsibility, effective stewardship, and alignment with enrollment, retention, and divisional priorities. Analyze budget trends, assess resource needs, and make recommendations that support departmental effectiveness and the residential student experience. Foster a culture of learning, belonging, collaboration, accountability, innovation, and professional growth of staff and student employees that advances the Division of Student Life’s priorities. Perform other duties as assigned. OTHER KEY RESPONSIBILITIES Support the College’s mission, vision and values, and contribute to the advancement of the College’s Strategic Plan. Demonstrate a strong commitment to continuous quality improvement. Demonstrate civil and inclusive behavior when interacting with staff, faculty, students and visitors to the College. Promote a flexible, collaborative and inclusive work and living environment and engage in educational opportunities to increase awareness and understanding of diversity and inclusion. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Customer service is critical to the success of any organization that deals with customers. Strong customer service skills can build retention and visibility as North Central College strives to distinguish itself from its competitors. An individual in this position must possess the proficiency, wherewithal and knowledge of principles and processes for providing high-quality customer and personal services to both internal and external customers of the College. The successful candidate will demonstrate the required knowledge, skills, and ability to: Demonstrate knowledge of student development theory, residential education, residential conduct administration, crisis response, and higher education best practices. Build productive relationships with students, families, faculty, staff, and campus partners while fostering a caring and inclusive community. Remain calm in crisis situations, exercise sound judgment, and effectively assist students in emotionally charged situations. Work effectively as a member of a staff where cooperation and teamwork are essential. Supervise and develop professional staff through collaborative leadership, coaching, accountability, and professional growth. Manage multiple priorities simultaneously while maintaining strong organizational effectiveness, flexibility, and attention to detail. Utilize assessment practices and data-informed decision making to support continuous improvement. Effectively manage student conduct matters utilizing educational, restorative, and developmental approaches. Demonstrate strong written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. Effectively utilize housing and case management systems, reporting tools, and related technologies, including ErezLife and Maxient, and apply sound financial and operational practices to support departmental effectiveness. Exercise emotional intelligence, discretion, and sound judgment when managing sensitive information and complex student situations. Distinguishing Characteristics The residential experience has a profound impact on student engagement, persistence, leadership development, academic success, and overall student well-being at a residential liberal arts institution. It is the responsibility of the Senior Director of Housing and Residential Education to foster safe, inclusive, and educationally purposeful communities that support learning and contribute to a positive residential experience for students. This position provides leadership for residential conduct processes, serves as co-chair of the College’s CARE Team, and contributes to divisional priorities through collaboration, assessment, and strategic planning. The duties of this position require evening and weekend work, including after-hours crisis management, emergency on-call responsibilities, and attendance at a variety of events. Education/Training Master’s degree in Higher Education, College Student Personnel, Student Affairs Administration, Counseling, or a related field required. Experience A minimum of seven years of progressively responsible professional experience in housing, residential education, student conduct, or student affairs administration within higher education required. Eight years of progressively responsible experience is preferred. Demonstrated experience supervising professional staff, leading residential operations, managing departmental budgets and resources, administering student conduct processes, managing complex student situations, and utilizing student information systems and technology platforms is required. Experience with housing management systems and case management platforms, including ErezLife and Maxient, is strongly preferred. Experience serving on behavioral intervention teams, participating in crisis response efforts, and utilizing assessment practices to inform decision making is strongly preferred. Experience at a residential liberal arts institution with a substantial residential population is strongly preferred. Licenses or Certifications Is This a Temporary Position? No Employment Conditions An offer of employment is contingent upon successful completion of the College’s background screening process. Number of Vacancies 1 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer. North Central College provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation, please notify the Office of Human Resource. Determinations on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Additional Information The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the candidate’s experience, qualifications, and training. North Central College provides a comprehensive benefits package, which includes: All-inclusive medical, dental and vision group insurance Health and dependent care savings plans Employer-paid life, AD&D and disability group insurance 403(b) retirement plan with employer contributions Employee assistance plan resources Tuition waiver for full-time employees, spouse and dependent children Generous paid time off plans, including 3 weeks vacation to start 15 paid holidays per year, including 5 days during NCC Winter Break Free access to campus health center, fitness and athletic facilities, and library Employee discount program Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Are you authorized to work in the US? YES NO Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for work authorization? YES NO Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Interest Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16080": {
      "title": "General Maintenance II - Electrician",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | General Maintenance II - Electrician Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help General Maintenance II - Electrician Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Posting Number S000983 Position Title General Maintenance II - Electrician Department Physical Plant Administration Division Facilities Planning and Operations Supervisor Title Director of Physical Plant Status Full Time Regular Salary Range $38/Hr. Position Summary General Maintenance II – Electrician is primarily responsible for the maintenance of electrical equipment and building wiring and lighting systems. This position is also responsible to ensure that assigned buildings, including furnishings, mechanical, plumbing and electrical systems therein, are functional on a continual basis. Description of Key Responsibilities Repair, install, replace, and test electrical circuits, equipment, and appliances using appropriate tools and testing instruments. Lay out, assemble, install, test, repair and adjust electrical fixtures, apparatus equipment, wiring, and new services to include panels, breakers and conduit. Inspect the exterior and appropriate interior areas of assigned buildings on a regular basis, including exterior lighting fixtures. Identify, prioritize and perform or report necessary maintenance activities. Promptly report safety and security concerns. Maintain regular contact with sources of work assignments (i.e. supervisor, assignment board, and the RT Tracker work order tracking system) so that necessary activities are carried out in a timely fashion. Promptly report completion of activities. Extensive electrical experience in servicing, adjusting, repairing, and replacing building components, for systems up to and including three-phase power, based on observed needs, work orders, preventive maintenance schedules, or other sources. Examples of typical activities are: advanced trouble-shooting skills of equipment and systems to determine causes of problems; repair or replace electrical fixtures and/or associated wiring, circuit breakers, transformers, disconnects, and responsible to replace burned-out or broken light bulbs and tubes (interior and exterior), as necessary. Ability to repair worn or damaged walls, floors, doors (including door hardware, cores and keys). Basic knowledge and ability to service; card readers, door operators and opener systems. Troubleshoot and repair electric controls, motor controls, lighting controls and programmable logic controls. Ability to repair blinds, window and hardware, repair damaged or malfunctioning washroom fixtures and associated piping; service and repair HVAC equipment power sources, refrigeration and fire protection systems; and seal water & gas leaks. Report the need for more extensive work by others, including outside contractors, to immediate supervisor. Advanced knowledge and skills required to carry out projects, which involve installations, renovations, remodeling, etc., as directed by supervisor, either individually or in a lead role overseeing other maintenance staff and student workers. Maintain building ingress/egress areas (i.e., sidewalks, stairways) in a serviceable condition by removing debris and snow and ice. Coordinate all activities with building users and contractors (i.e. food service, cleaning services) so as to maximize efficiency and minimize disruption to building activities/services. Flexibility to be assigned to temporarily work at other locations to substitute for personnel not available or to supplement personnel during times of unusually heavy workload. Install and/or repair exterior building appurtenances such as fences and railings. Move or assist in moving equipment, furniture, materials and supplies to/from storage areas or between locations. Open (unlock) and/or secure (lock) buildings as required. Travel to vendors to pick up materials and supplies. Perform other job-related duties and tasks as assigned. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Ability to communicate in the English language, both orally and written, in order to receive and/or disburse assignments and instructions, understand written procedures and cautions, prepare written requests, record work completed, etc. Ability to utilize basic mathematical skills necessary for calculating quantities, preparing estimates, etc. Ability to interpret blue prints; work from sketches, verbal instructions, and apply NEC through the full range of commercial and industrial. Knowledge of electrical systems including; planning additions or modifications to electrical service panels, proper electrical grounding, and electrical safety requirements Ex. Lock-out/Tag-out ( LOTO ). Ability to accurately interpret drawings and schematics representing electrical, mechanical, architectural and structural systems. Ability to sketch or provide basic drawings or floor plans for projects. Ability to safely and effectively transport (which may involve lifting up to 50 pounds) and utilize materials and equipment used in facility servicing and maintenance and to work safely from lifts, ladders, scaffolds, and other elevated positions. Advanced ability to perform a variety of building maintenance tasks requiring knowledge and skills working with electrical and mechanical systems (including HVAC ), plumbing, carpentry, masonry and painting projects. On occasion, work outdoors in inclement weather and to work other-than-normal and, at times, longer than normal hours. Ability to carry out work efficiently and in a manner, which will minimize interference with building operations. Ability to effectively communicate with, and relate positively to, a variety of people representing various building users. Ability to quickly grasp knowledge of the College organization and operation so as to contribute positively. Ability to use power tools in an appropriate and safe manner. Demonstrate civil and inclusive behavior when interacting with staff, faculty, students and visitors to the College. Promote a flexible, collaborative and inclusive work and living environment and engage in educational opportunities to increase awareness and understanding of diversity and inclusion. Distinguishing Characteristics The condition of the College’s buildings has a profound effect on the overall appearance of the campus, as well as on the daily lives of all who live, study and work there. Perhaps most striking is the impact this function has on the quality of life of students, particularly those who reside on campus. The College’s work force which is responsible for buildings and grounds maintenance is generally made up of individuals who each perform a wide variety of duties, and whose work strongly affects a diverse group of users of the facilities. Thus, employees in this area are expected to be multi-skilled, flexible, and able to work cooperatively with each other (to the extent of doing work of subordinates when necessary) and with other members of the campus community, often without direct supervision. Because of the nature of the work, reliable and regular attendance is essential. Because individuals in this position must interact regularly with a wide variety of members of the campus community, as well as, on occasion, inspectors from City and State agencies and the College’s insurance carrier; uniforms, furnished by the College, must be worn at times determined by the position supervisor. Individuals in this position may be required to work at times that are other than, or in addition to, normal hours and days, including responding at any time to severe weather and other emergency conditions.Also, individuals in this position can expect to participate in “on-call” rotation during nights and weekends for specified periods. Individuals must be available by telephone, to report to work, fit for duty during these scheduled rotation periods. Education/Training A high school diploma or GED is required. Electrician certification preferred. Experience Extensive electrical experience is required for this position. A minimum of 5 years of electrical experience and a minimum of 5 years of experience in building maintenance, construction or related field is required. Licenses or Certifications A valid driver’s license and acceptable driving record are required. Is This a Temporary Position? No Employment Conditions An offer of employment is contingent upon successful completion of the College’s background screening process. Number of Vacancies 1 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to increasing the diversity of our workforce. NCC provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process, please notify the Office of Human Resources. Determinations for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Additional Information The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the candidate’s experience, qualifications, and training. North Central College provides a comprehensive benefits package, which includes: All-inclusive medical, dental and vision group insurance Health and dependent care savings plans Employer-paid life, AD&D and disability group insurance 403(b) retirement plan with employer contributions Employee assistance plan resources Tuition waiver for full-time employees, spouse and dependent children Generous paid time off plans, including 3 weeks vacation to start 15 paid holidays per year, including 5 days during NCC Winter Break Free access to campus health center, fitness and athletic facilities, and library Employee discount program Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you authorized to work in the US? YES NO * Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for work authorization? YES NO Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Interest Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16078": {
      "title": "Laundry Attendant Student-Worker",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | Laundry Attendant Student-Worker Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Laundry Attendant Student-Worker Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Federal Work Study Eligible? Yes Posting Number ST000938 Position Title Laundry Attendant Student-Worker Department Athletic Training Division Student Affairs and Athletics Supervisor Robby Bates Supervisor Title Asst. Men's Wrestling Coach Description of Job Duties To ensure that athletic team laundry is completed in timely manner on a near daily basis in its entirety. 1. Properly wash, dry and fold the desired uniforms and linens as established by the supervisor. Individuals must adhere to recommended manufacturer cleaning and care instructions. 2. Ensure that the laundering of uniforms and linens are completed in a timely manner. 3. Ensure cleanliness of the surrounding work space, including the cleaning of lint traps, sweeping of floors, and disposal of refuse. 4. Establish a dependable work schedule based on attendance and punctuality. 5. Perform other related duties as assigned. 6. Report any malfunctioning equipment such as washer or dryers. 7. Maintain an adequate amount of supplies by communicating inventory to a supervisor. Do the job duties for this position include any driving requirements? No Job Qualifications Must be enrolled as an NCC student Will this position be working with minors? No Approximate number of work hours per week Varies Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer. NCC provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process, please notify the Office of Human Resources at 630-637-5757 or humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu Determinations for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Open Date 08/06/2025 Close Date Open Until Filled No Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you currently enrolled as a North Central College student? Yes No * Please select the answer that best describes your student work authorization: I am authorized to work for any US employer and can provide documentation, if hired. I am an international student with a US social security number authorized for campus work. I am an international student and will need to apply for a US social security number, if hired. I cannot provide US work authorization. Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Interest Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
