    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16057": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor of Media Studies",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | Adjunct Instructor of Media Studies Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Adjunct Instructor of Media Studies Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Posting Number F000497 Position Title Adjunct Instructor of Media Studies Department Communication & Media Studies Division Academic Affairs Faculty Status Per Course Part-time Faculty Supervisor Title Chair of Communication and Media Studies Status Per Course Part-time Faculty Tenure Track No Salary Range $3840 - $4000 Position Summary Adjunct Media Studies Faculty, Spring 2027 The Department of Communication and Media Studies in the College of Arts and Sciences at North Central College invites applications for adjunct faculty to teach one or two sections of our introduction to media course for the spring semester of 2027. COMM 185/Global Media in a Digital Age is a survey of the development, structure, economics and regulation of major media with a special emphasis on digital communication. The sociological and cultural impact of media on society and civic life is a central theme of the course. This introductory course is required for all Communication and Media Studies majors and is also a popular general education course for students across the College. We are seeking qualified instructors for the following sections for the spring semester: Monday, Wednesday, and Friday 8:00AM-9:05AM Monday, Wednesday, and Friday 9:20AM-10:25AM All instruction will take place in-person at our Naperville, IL campus. Spring semester runs from January 11 to May 5, 2027. The successful candidate will have a Master’s degree in Media Studies, Communication, Journalism or a closely related field with relevant coursework. Candidates with successful college-level teaching experience of media courses are preferred. North Central College values diversity, equity and inclusion and strongly encourages candidates who would enrich the diversity of the campus community to apply. Experience working with diverse student populations and communities is highly desirable. Founded in 1861, North Central College is a nationally recognized institution of about 3000 students, located in Naperville, IL, a thriving suburb just west of Chicago. The College is in a period of growth with the addition of a $60 million Science Center (2017), and a new Health Sciences and Engineering building (2020). North Central College is within walking distance of the Naperville Metra station and is next to downtown Naperville. The Naperville area is home to many excellent school districts and Naperville, IL has been ranked as one of the best places to live by Money magazine. For more information, please visit: http://www.noctrl.edu . North Central College is an EEO Employer. For more information, please visit: https://www.northcentralcollege.edu/academic-affairs/prospective-and-incoming-faculty Interested candidates should complete and submit an application with all required documentation online at: https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16055 For further information, contact Amy Buxbaum Chair, Communication and Media Studies at agbuxbaum@noctrl.edu. Employment Conditions An offer of employment is contingent upon successful completion of the College’s background screening process. Number of Vacancies 1 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to increasing the diversity of our workforce. North Central College provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation, please notify the Office of Human Resource. Determinations on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Additional Information The budgeted salary range for this position is $3840 – $4000. The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the selected candidate’s experience and qualifications, including equivalent years in rank, training, and field or discipline; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you authorized to work in the US? YES NO * Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for work authorization? YES NO Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Interest Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16047": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track, Criminology (Open Rank)",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | Tenure-Track, Criminology (Open Rank) Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Tenure-Track, Criminology (Open Rank) Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Posting Number F000496 Position Title Tenure-Track, Criminology (Open Rank) Department Sociology, Anthropology & Criminology Division Academic Affairs Faculty Status Full-time Tenure-Track Faculty Supervisor Title Chair, Department of Sociology, Criminology, & Anthropology Status Full-time Tenure-Track Faculty Tenure Track Yes Salary Range Position Summary The Department of Sociology, Criminology, & Anthropology in the College of Arts and Sciences at North Central College invites enthusiastic educators for a tenure-track position in Criminology beginning August 2027. We are particularly interested in candidates whose teaching and scholarship emphasize the social, cultural, political, or structural dimensions of crime, law, and justice. Our collaborative department is dedicated to engaged teaching, experiential learning, undergraduate research, and faculty-student mentorship. Faculty are encouraged to integrate their expertise into teaching and contribute to a vibrant intellectual community on campus and beyond. This 9.5-month, tenure-track appointment carries a teaching load of 20 credit hours per academic year, typically met by teaching five 4-credit courses across the fall and spring semesters. The position is on campus. The duties in this role include: Teach undergraduate courses in the criminology curriculum, including introductory, core, and upper-level offerings consistent with departmental needs and the candidate’s expertise. Foster student learning through effective instruction, meaningful assessment, and constructive feedback. Mentor undergraduate students individually and in groups, including guidance on senior capstone theses and internships. Involve students in research when feasible. Support an inclusive, engaging learning environment that promotes student success. Develop and support experiential and community-engaged learning opportunities. Maintain an active scholarship agenda appropriate for a teaching-focused liberal arts institution, including research, publications, and professional presentations. Contribute to curriculum development, assessment, and continuous improvement of the program and department. Participate in departmental, college, and professional service, including committee work, meetings, and other institutional responsibilities. Stay current in the discipline through ongoing professional development and participation in relevant scholarly and professional organizations. Basic Qualifications: Ph.D. in Criminology, Sociology, or a closely related discipline required by August 2027. A J.D. alone does not meet the qualifications for this position. Demonstrated ability to integrate criminological and sociological perspectives in teaching and research. Evidence of effective undergraduate teaching. A clear research agenda with demonstrated potential for peer-reviewed scholarship in criminology. Commitment to excellence in teaching, mentoring, scholarship, and service. Preferred Qualifications: Expertise in critical criminology, comparative criminology, or related areas that complement departmental strengths. Experience engaging undergraduate students in research, creative activities, or other scholarly work. Experience developing innovative courses or curricular initiatives that broaden and strengthen departmental offerings. The budgeted salary range for this position is: Assistant Professor: $62,000 – $67,000 Associate Professor: $72,000 – $78,000 Professor: $83,000 – $88,000 About NCC : Founded in 1861, North Central College is a selective, comprehensive liberal arts institution of about 3000 students, located 28 miles west of Chicago in Naperville. The College is in a period of growth with the addition of a $60 million Science Center (2017), and a new Health Sciences and Engineering building (2020). The college is within walking distance of the Naperville Metra station and is next to downtown Naperville. The Naperville area is home to many excellent school districts and Naperville, IL has been ranked as one of the best places to live by Money magazine. North Central College values diversity, equity and inclusion and strongly encourages candidates who would enrich the diversity of the campus community to apply. Experience working with diverse student populations and communities is highly desirable. Application Window: Open date: September 2, 2026 Initial review date: October 2, 2026 Apply by initial review date to ensure full consideration by the committee. Final date: November 17, 2026 For more information, please visit: https://www.northcentralcollege.edu/academic-affairs/prospective-and-incoming-faculty Interested candidates should complete and submit an application with all required documentation online at: https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16045 For further information, contact Raleigh Blasdell, Chair, Department of Sociology, Criminology, and Anthropology, at rblasdell@noctrl.edu. Employment Conditions An offer of employment is contingent upon successful completion of the College’s background screening process. Number of Vacancies 1 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to increasing the diversity of our workforce. North Central College provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation, please notify the Office of Human Resource. Determinations on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Additional Information The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the selected candidate’s experience and qualifications, including equivalent years in rank, training, and field or discipline; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. North Central College provides a comprehensive benefits package, which includes: All-inclusive medical, dental and vision group insurance Health and dependent care savings plans Employer-paid life and AD&D insurance 403(b) retirement plan with employer contributions Employee assistance plan resources Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you authorized to work in the US? YES NO * Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for work authorization? YES NO Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter/Letter of Interest Teaching Philosophy Diversity Statement Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/16038": {
      "title": "Director of Marketing",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | Director of Marketing Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Director of Marketing Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Posting Number S000981 Position Title Director of Marketing Department Institutional Communication Division Institutional Communication Supervisor Title Vice President for Communication and Marketing Status Full Time Regular Salary Range $70,000 - $95,000 Position Summary This is a director-level opportunity for a creative, strategic, hands-on marketer to build institutional marketing capacity within an established communications, content, and creative services team at a mission-driven college. The Director of Marketing serves as a key member of the Office of Institutional Communication and Marketing ( OICM ) and provides leadership for the College’s institutional marketing, brand visibility, and top-of-funnel audience development efforts. Reporting to the Vice President for Communication and Marketing, the Director brings strategic marketing expertise, creative thinking, and hands-on execution to the work of expanding awareness of the College and strengthening the institution’s presence among prospective students and families, alumni, donors, community partners, and other external audiences. The Director will be a representative of the College’s brand identity and visibility, working in close collaboration with colleagues who lead institutional voice, messaging, media relations, internal communications, editorial content, social media, presidential voice, and creative services. Success in this position will require a highly collaborative, team-oriented leader who can bring marketing discipline and fresh perspective to an institutional strategic priority: to position North Central College for the future given current challenges facing higher education. The Director will initially play a central role in two major institutional priorities: leading the marketing strategy for a comprehensive redesign of the College website and developing a broad top-of-funnel marketing and audience development campaign designed to expand the College’s exposure and strengthen its reputation in key markets. The position will oversee the website as a gateway institutional marketing platform, helping ensure that it effectively presents the College’s value, advances brand visibility, supports audience engagement, and reflects best practices in user experience, accessibility, search, content strategy, and digital performance. The ideal candidate will combine the creativity and market orientation of an experienced marketing professional with the judgment, adaptability, and mission focus needed to succeed in higher education. This position is an excellent opportunity for someone with agency, corporate, healthcare, nonprofit or higher education experience to promote the College’s work, which is to communicate the value of a transformative educational experience in ways that are authentic, compelling and strategically aligned. Description of Key Responsibilities Provide hands-on leadership for the College’s institutional marketing, brand visibility, and top-of-funnel audience development efforts, ensuring alignment with institutional priorities and the direction of the Vice President for Communication and Marketing. Serve as a strategic marketing partner to the Vice President for Communication and Marketing, contributing ideas, analysis, recommendations, and executional support for initiatives that strengthen the College’s visibility, reputation, and competitive position. Lead the development and implementation of institutional marketing strategies designed to expand awareness of the College among key external audiences important to the College’s mission and future. Develop and execute broad top-of-funnel marketing campaigns that increase the College’s exposure in key markets and communicate its distinctive value in a compelling, authentic, and mission-aligned manner. Steward the College’s institutional brand identity in close collaboration with colleagues responsible for institutional voice, messaging, storytelling, editorial content, creative services, and communications strategy. Provide marketing leadership for the comprehensive redesign of the College website, including marketing strategy, audience pathways, user experience, vendor coordination, implementation support, post-launch optimization, and ongoing strategy. Oversee the College website as a primary institutional marketing platform, using audience insights, analytics, content strategy, accessibility standards, search best practices, and digital performance measures to improve visibility, engagement, and effectiveness. Collaborate with colleagues in admissions, advancement, athletics, academic affairs, student affairs, and other areas to ensure institutional marketing efforts support broad College priorities while remaining coordinated with unit-specific communications and marketing needs. Partner effectively with colleagues in institutional communication to ensure that marketing strategy, brand visibility, institutional voice, storytelling, media relations, internal communications, creative services, and editorial content work together in a coordinated and mutually reinforcing way. Develop creative briefs, campaign plans, audience strategies, project scopes, and other planning tools that translate institutional priorities into actionable marketing work. Manage relationships with external vendors, agencies, consultants, web partners, and other service providers, ensuring quality, timeliness, brand alignment, and fiscal responsibility. Use research, data, analytics, and market insights to inform marketing strategy, evaluate campaign effectiveness, and recommend adjustments that improve reach, engagement, and performance. Monitor market trends, audience behavior, competitive positioning, and emerging marketing practices, including appropriate uses of artificial intelligence and digital tools, to strengthen the College’s marketing effectiveness. Support the development of processes, workflows, and project management practices that allow marketing work to be prioritized, coordinated, executed, and assessed effectively. Contribute directly to marketing planning, campaign development, project management, vendor coordination, and campaign execution as a hands-on member of the team. Represent the Office of Institutional Communication and Marketing on College-wide initiatives and committees as appropriate. Perform other duties as assigned. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Significant, progressively responsible experience in marketing, brand strategy, digital marketing, campaign development, or a related field, with demonstrated ability to develop and execute strategies that expand awareness, visibility, engagement, and reputation. Strong understanding of institutional marketing, brand positioning, audience development, and top-of-funnel strategy in a complex organizational environment. Strong understanding of brand identity, brand architecture, creative strategy, campaign development, and the ways visual identity, messaging, audience experience, and institutional reputation work together. Demonstrated ability to serve as both a strategic thinker and hands-on practitioner, contributing directly to marketing planning, campaign development, project management, vendor coordination, content development, and execution. Experience developing marketing campaigns across multiple channels, including digital, web, paid, earned, owned, and shared platforms. Demonstrated understanding of website strategy as a central marketing function, including user experience, audience pathways, content strategy, search performance, accessibility, analytics, content governance, and ongoing optimization. Ability to use research, data, analytics, market insights, and performance metrics to inform strategy, evaluate effectiveness, and recommend adjustments that improve reach, engagement, and performance. Strong project management skills, including the ability to manage complex, cross-functional projects; coordinate timelines, deliverables, and partners; and maintain momentum from strategy through execution. Ability to develop and use creative briefs, campaign plans, audience strategies, project scopes, and other planning tools that support clear direction, efficient execution, and strong outcomes. Demonstrated ability to manage relationships with external vendors, agencies, consultants, designers, web partners, and other service providers with attention to quality, brand alignment, budget, deadlines, and accountability. Strong creative judgment and the ability to evaluate marketing concepts, visual direction, campaign ideas, web experiences, and audience-facing materials for strategic fit and effectiveness. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to articulate marketing strategy, present recommendations, and collaborate effectively with colleagues who lead institutional voice, storytelling, media relations, internal communications, editorial content, and creative services. Strong interpersonal skills, including the ability to build trust, collaborate across functions, influence without direct authority, and work productively with senior leadership, faculty, staff, students, external partners, and community stakeholders. Ability to operate effectively in a mission-driven, service-oriented environment, with a strong appreciation for the distinctive nature of higher education. Familiarity with emerging marketing practices and technologies, including appropriate uses of artificial intelligence and digital tools to improve marketing effectiveness, workflow, and audience engagement. Demonstrated professionalism, integrity, sound judgment, and discretion. Distinguishing Characteristics The Director of Marketing is a newly created position within the Office of Institutional Communication and Marketing, established to strengthen the College’s institutional marketing capacity and expand its visibility among key external audiences, while complementing the communications, storytelling, editorial, and creative expertise already present in the office. The Director serves as a hands-on marketing expert and strategic contributor to the Vice President for Communication and Marketing. The position requires the ability to move fluidly between strategy and execution: developing marketing concepts, shaping campaign direction, guiding digital and web strategy, managing projects and partners, and helping translate institutional priorities into effective marketing approaches. The person in this role will work with an experienced communications team whose members lead institutional voice, storytelling, media relations, internal communications, editorial content, and creative services. Building strong, respectful, and productive partnerships with colleagues across the office and throughout the College, the Director of Marketing will ensure that brand visibility, marketing strategy, and institutional messaging work together in a coordinated and mission-aligned way. The position is also distinguished by its responsibility for the College’s website as a primary institutional marketing platform. The Director will provide leadership for the marketing strategy, user experience, audience pathways, vendor coordination, and ongoing performance of the website, with particular attention to how the site advances visibility, reputation, engagement, and institutional priorities. This role is well suited for a creative, collaborative, and highly organized marketing professional who brings strong judgment, initiative, and executional discipline to generate awareness and interest in a competitive environment while appreciating the transformative impact of mission-driven higher education. Education/Training Bachelor’s degree required in marketing, communications, public relations, journalism, business, digital media, or a related field. A master’s degree or other advanced training in marketing, business, communications, digital strategy, or a related field is preferred. Experience A minimum of seven years of progressively responsible experience in marketing, brand strategy, digital marketing, campaign development, website strategy, or a related field is required; ten or more years is preferred. The successful candidate will have demonstrated experience developing and executing marketing strategies that expand visibility, strengthen brand awareness, deepen audience engagement, and improve marketing performance. Experience in agency, corporate, healthcare, nonprofit, higher education, or other complex, mission-driven environments is relevant, with higher education experience preferred but not required. Licenses or Certifications Certification: Professional certifications or advanced training in marketing, digital marketing, analytics, project management, web strategy, accessibility, artificial intelligence, or related fields are preferred but not required. Is This a Temporary Position? No Employment Conditions An offer of employment is contingent upon successful completion of the College’s background screening process. Number of Vacancies 1 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to increasing the diversity of our workforce. NCC provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process, please notify the Office of Human Resources. Determinations for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Additional Information The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the candidate’s experience, qualifications, and training. North Central College provides a comprehensive benefits package, which includes: All-inclusive medical, dental and vision group insurance Health and dependent care savings plans Employer-paid life, AD&D and disability group insurance 403(b) retirement plan with employer contributions Employee assistance plan resources Tuition waiver for full-time employees, spouse and dependent children Generous paid time off plans, including 3 weeks vacation to start 15 paid holidays per year, including 5 days during NCC Winter Break Free access to campus health center, fitness and athletic facilities, and library Employee discount program Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you authorized to work in the US? YES NO * Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for work authorization? YES NO Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Interest Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/15957": {
      "title": "Director of Sponsored Research and Programs",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | Director of Sponsored Research and Programs Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Director of Sponsored Research and Programs Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Posting Number S000966 Position Title Director of Sponsored Research and Programs Department Academic Affairs Division Academic Affairs Supervisor Title Provost Status Full Time Regular Salary Range $75,000-$85,000 Position Summary The Director of Sponsored Research and Programs is responsible for expanding the portfolio of external grants that supports the College’s academic programs, research enterprise, and creative projects by providing assistance with proposal development for faculty across all academic departments. This position will oversee the complete lifecycle of academic and research grants, including operations of both pre-award and post-award, non-financial administration. The Director of Sponsored Research and Programs is also responsible for maintaining and updating institutional policies and procedures to ensure compliance with public and private funding agencies. The position cultivates collaborative relationships with faculty, administrators, and other key campus staff to stay informed of current programmatic activities, scholarly and creative interests, and upcoming initiatives, and then matches such institutional activities and priorities with potential funding opportunities. Description of Key Responsibilities Identify and recommend grant opportunities that align with the College’s strategic plan and work with the Provost and Deans to identify faculty who could potentially serve as PIs. Continue developing a culture of grant seeking among faculty and make recommendations for strategic investments that build capacity to support faculty scholarship and creative work. Work with the Center for the Advancement of Faculty Excellence ( CAFE ) to advance the mission of supporting faculty scholarship by developing and facilitating professional development opportunities on the topics of proposal writing and grants management. Provide guidance to PIs on proposal development strategies including the facilitation of outreach to program officers at granting organizations. Coordinate organizational meetings with PIs to discuss funder guidelines, generate ideas, assist with concept development of programs/projects, and ensure compliance with institutional policies. Provide support on the development of grant applications including but not limited to editing sections of the proposal, helping to prepare budgets and budget narratives, and compiling additional documentation required for submission. Review and edit all grant proposals for clarity, conformance with funding program priorities, and route through the appropriate channels for approval to submit. Serve as the designated Authorized Organizational Representative ( AOR ) responsible for uploading application documents to granting agency portals and submitting completed applications. Facilitate grant award kick-off meetings with the Grants Accountant, PIs, and other relevant College stakeholders to ensure all parties understand their role in award management. Work with the Business Office in setup of new awards including the creation of general ledger accounts for each grant based on approved budget and distribution of indirect cost recoveries per the College’s policy. Review grant agreements and subawards and, if required, route to College leadership for their review and signature. Monitor grant report deadlines, review and edit progress reports for PIs, work with the Grants Accountant to produce financial reports, and coordinate timely submission with PIs. Maintain and update institutional policies and procedures of grant management (e.g., time and effort certification, financial conflict of interest, responsible conduct of research, allowable costs) in collaboration with the Business Office and other campus stakeholders and in compliance with funding agencies and federal regulations (i.e., Uniform Guidance). Develop and maintain a resources webpage for the Office of Sponsored Research & Programs on The Hub that includes relevant institutional procedures and policies for grant seekers. Track and report progress to goals on proposal submissions and awards secured. Serve as an ex-officio member of the Faculty Development & Recognition Committee. Other duties as appropriately assigned. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Demonstrated interpersonal and team skills. Strong interpersonal skills to develop and maintain cooperative and productive working relationships with diverse college and external communities. Excellent communication skills, including writing, listening and presentation. This position requires independent judgment and decision making and ability to work well independently, as well as in a team environment. Must be able to manage multiple projects simultaneously, set priorities, effectively manage time and undertake long-term projects. Extensive knowledge and experience with Windows, Excel, Word and PowerPoint software. Related professional experience with proven track record of securing external funds, and successful experience in sponsored programs administration and other related higher education experience. Contract and grant solicitation and administration with knowledge of potential individual, private, institutional and governmental sources of contract and grant opportunities for a historic, private liberal arts college. Knowledge of community sustainability issues and the role of the College in addressing the educational and economic development needs of the community. An understanding of the political, economic and community development issues facing small private colleges nationally and Illinois higher education in particular. Demonstrated commitment to the employment and development of an ethnically and culturally diverse staff. Distinguishing Characteristics An individual in this position must possess the proficiency, wherewithal and knowledge of principles and processes for providing high‐quality service to both internal and external constituents of the College. More specifically, this position will help build capacity and infrastructure for meeting the sponsored research and project needs of the faculty and the institution. Education/Training Bachelor’s degree required; master’s degree or higher is preferred Experience A total of at least 5-7 years of experience within the following areas: Experience in a grants office or and office of sponsored research/programs in higher education or non-profit institution. Administrative experience in higher education. Expertise in government and private sector funding opportunities in support of higher education and research; expertise in federal, state, local and institutional regulations and policies governing administration of higher education research programs, grants and contracts required. Licenses or Certifications Is This a Temporary Position? No Employment Conditions An offer of employment is contingent upon successful completion of the College’s background screening process. Number of Vacancies 1 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to increasing the diversity of our workforce. NCC provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process, please notify the Office of Human Resources. Determinations for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Additional Information The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the candidate’s experience, qualifications, and training. A hybrid work arrangement may be available for this position. Potential hybrid work arrangements can be discussed with qualified candidates during the interview process, taking into consideration the needs of the candidate, the responsibilities of the position, and the operational needs of the department. North Central College provides a comprehensive benefits package, which includes: All-inclusive medical, dental and vision group insurance Health and dependent care savings plans Employer-paid life, AD&D and disability group insurance 403(b) retirement plan with employer contributions Employee assistance plan resources Tuition waiver for full-time employees, spouse and dependent children Generous paid time off plans, including 3 weeks vacation to start 15 paid holidays per year, including 5 days during NCC Winter Break Free access to campus health center, fitness and athletic facilities, and library Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you authorized to work in the US? YES NO * Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for work authorization? YES NO Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Interest Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/15950": {
      "title": "Internship Coordinator &amp; Career Readiness Specialist",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | Internship Coordinator &amp; Career Readiness Specialist Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Internship Coordinator & Career Readiness Specialist Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Posting Number S000976 Position Title Internship Coordinator & Career Readiness Specialist Department Center for Career and Professional Development Division Academic Affairs Supervisor Title Executive Director, Center for Career and Professional Development Status Full Time Regular Salary Range $50,000 - $57,000 Position Summary The Internship Coordinator & Career Readiness Specialist within the North Central College Center for Career and Professional Development ( CCPD ) supports the College’s commitment to career readiness by coordinating the administration of credit-bearing internships and advancing institutional cocurricular assessment efforts. The position is responsible for overseeing internship processes and policies, supporting assessment of the All-College Career Readiness Requirement ( ACR ), collaborating on career outcomes initiatives, maintaining accurate internship and assessment data, and providing data-informed insights that support continuous improvement, institutional planning, and student success. Reporting to the Executive Director, the Internship Coordinator & Career Readiness Specialist will serve as a member of the CCPD professional staff, supporting students, employers, and faculty in further advancing all career readiness efforts at North Central College. The successful candidate will oversee the administration of credited internship opportunities, supporting students, faculty, and employers throughout the internship approval process for experiences completed both in fulfillment of the ACR and independently of the requirement. The Internship Coordinator & Career Readiness Specialist is tasked with advancing experiential learning and student development in conjunction with students’ curricular experiences. Description of Key Responsibilities Serve as the College’s primary contact for all matters related to internships, including policies, procedures, and best practices, while leading the development, implementation, and continuous improvement of internship processes, resources, and documentation. Oversee student internship approvals and ensure internship experiences meet institutional and Career Readiness Requirement standards. The successful candidate will be expected communicate with students through stages of the process via Handshake, email, and in person. Provide student and administrative support for the ongoing implementation and execution of the All-College Career Readiness Requirement ( ACR ). Assess the effectiveness of the ACR in collaboration with the Executive Director and Assistant Provost and recommend improvements. Collect and use student feedback to continuously improve and innovate career center offerings. Host and facilitate workshops and informational sessions to help students pursue internships opportunities and the ACR . Support the Executive Director in engaging students and alumni with the First Destination Survey and Reporting to gather post-graduation outcomes and insights on behalf of the College. Collaborate with faculty, campus partners, and the Executive Director to develop assessment strategies that demonstrate the impact of career readiness initiatives. Respond to institutional requests for career outcomes, assessment, and internship data while ensuring data accuracy and integrity. Maintain accurate records and implement quality assurance practices for internship, career outcomes, and assessment data. Conduct one-on-one career coaching appointments and support regular drop-in hours with students as needed to support departmental operations during peak demand periods on topics including career exploration, networking, interview preparation, internship and job search, graduate school planning, and other career-related needs. Partner with the Associate Director of Employer Relations to expand and strengthen internship opportunities for students. Serve as a member of the College Career Consortium of Illinois ( CCCI ) and assume leadership positions as directed. Perform other related duties and tasks as appropriately assigned. To execute these duties successfully, the Internship Coordinator & Career Readiness Specialist must be able to work well with all constituents, including students, parents, faculty, staff, alumni, and employers. The Internship Coordinator & Career Readiness Specialist must be an enthusiastic and highly-visible spokesperson for the College both on- and off-campus. The Internship Coordinator & Career Readiness Specialist will represent the College and CCPD at professional meetings and conferences, actively participating in local, regional, and national associations to stay current with industry trends and best practices. This position will require occasional work outside of normal business hours and days, including attendance at evening and weekend events as needed. Travel, including local and regional site visits, and national conference/association participation, is also expected. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Ability to perform administrative functions with a high degree of ethics, integrity and professionalism. This includes the ability to organize, manage and maintain highly confidentiality electronic and written communication, being mindful of sensitive information and FERPA laws. Articulates an understanding of societal trust placed in the program professions and the relationship-based vision of the program. Ability to continuously demonstrate outstanding interpersonal skills in dealing with faculty, staff, students and with others, both on the telephone and in person. Ability to work cooperatively with others and capacity to work independently. Ability to communicate in the English language, both orally and in writing, with particular emphasis on the ability to produce documents that are attractively and professionally formatted and present proper spelling, grammatical usage and punctuation. Effectively use the most advanced version of word processing, Excel spreadsheet and database software, PowerPoint, administrative computing system and content management system software for webpage development and maintenance. This includes a willingness to explore new technological solutions to office needs and to anticipate new ways of supporting strategic goals. Ability to manage multiple activities concurrently in a high‐volume environment, to continually monitor their status and to prioritize activities so that deadlines are met, and overall office effectiveness is maximized. These characteristics require an individual who is well‐organized, detail oriented and flexible. Knowledge of the College’s mission, organization, operation, performance expectations, and culture, including a strong grasp of the “big picture” and details required of maintaining an accredited program. Distinguishing Characteristics The Center for Career & Professional Development ( CCPD ) is a comprehensive career center serving North Central students as they navigate all aspects of their personal and professional career journey. The CCPD team also assists employers and external stakeholders to connect with our campus and recruit talent for internships and jobs. The CCPD team collaborates with faculty members to integrate career content into their curricular experiences. The CCPD plays a critical and visible role on North Central’s campus and in the broader community. Strategically aligned with the Division of Academic Affairs, the CCPD is predominantly student-focused with insights in sustaining and advancing strategic partnerships with faculty, staff, employers, alumni, and various other stakeholders. In fall 2022, the College launched a four-credit-hour All-College Career Readiness Requirement ( ACR ) for all undergraduate students that will include a two credit-hour career-prep course and a complementary two credit-hour field-based career readiness experience. To support this college-wide requirement, the CCPD team has built and delivered career activities, online modules and resources for faculty members to effectively integrate career readiness into their curricular experiences. The Internship Coordinator & Career Readiness Specialist will play an important role in continued innovation of career readiness curricular content and the advancement of cross-campus collaborations with academic departments, faculty, and student support staff. Education/Training Bachelor’s degree required. Master’s degree preferred. Experience 1 – 2 year(s) of experience in higher education, career services, academic advising, student affairs, assessment, institutional research, data analysis, or a related field. Licenses or Certifications Is This a Temporary Position? No Employment Conditions An offer of employment is contingent upon successful completion of the College’s background screening process. Number of Vacancies 1 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to increasing the diversity of our workforce. NCC provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process, please notify the Office of Human Resources. Determinations for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Additional Information The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the candidate’s experience, qualifications, and training. North Central College provides a comprehensive benefits package, which includes: All-inclusive medical, dental and vision group insurance Health and dependent care savings plans Employer-paid life, AD&D and disability group insurance 403(b) retirement plan with employer contributions Employee assistance plan resources Tuition waiver for full-time employees, spouse and dependent children Generous paid time off plans, including 3 weeks vacation to start 15 paid holidays per year, including 5 days during NCC Winter Break Free access to campus health center, fitness and athletic facilities, and library Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you authorized to work in the US? YES NO * Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for work authorization? YES NO Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Interest Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/15943": {
      "title": "Director of Enrollment Operations and CRM",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | Director of Enrollment Operations and CRM Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Director of Enrollment Operations and CRM Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Posting Number S000975 Position Title Director of Enrollment Operations and CRM Department Enrollment Marketing Division Enrollment Management and Marketing Supervisor Title Vice President for Enrollment Strategy Status Full Time Regular Salary Range $70,000 - $80,000 Position Summary The Director of Enrollment Operations and CRM leads the strategic development, optimization, and management of North Central College’s enrollment technology infrastructure, data systems, processes, and data analysis across all entry types (undergraduate and graduate). Reporting directly to the Vice President for Enrollment Strategy and partnering closely with the Director of CRM Strategy and Institutional Insights, this role serves as enrollment management’s primary system administrator and strategist for Slate. The Director manages critical, high-volume data bridges—specifically between Slate, Centralized Application Services ( CAS ) platforms, other external platforms/vendors, and Ellucian Colleague—ensuring a seamless flow of data. This leader supervises a team dedicated to application processing, communication infrastructure, data integrity, and data analysis, transforming complex technical systems into high-efficiency workflows that support the institution’s enrollment goals. Description of Key Responsibilities Core Responsibilities 1. CRM Strategy and Slate System Administration (35%) Comprehensive System Partnership: Act as enrollment management’s primary Slate leader, partnering with the Director of CRM Strategy and Institutional Insights on database architecture, configuration, user permissions, and release management for all enrollment types. Unified Workflow Optimization: Build, refine, and maintain distinct application workflows, Reader bins, and conditional evaluation forms tailored to the varying needs of undergraduate and graduate programs. Rule Governance and Automation: Maintain clean rule architecture using exclusivity groups. Automate checklist mapping, document processing, and bin movement to minimize manual processing touches. Environment Testing: Assist in systemic updates, configuration changes, and new feature deployments using Time Warp and test environments prior to production release. 2. Prospective Student and Influencer Communication Architecture (20%) Omnichannel Communications: Technical design, execution, and automation of communication protocols using Slate Deliver (email, SMS , and print) targeting prospective students and their key influencers (parents, families, employers, high school counselors and community college counselors). Audience Segmentation: Leverage Configurable Joins and custom fields to build complex population segments, ensuring highly targeted messaging based on student type, academic interest, and influencer relationship. Dynamic and Portal Experiences: Develop, maintain, and test custom Slate Portals and dynamic content blocks that serve personalized enrollment checklist steps and engagement opportunities to both students and their influencers. Protocol Quality Assurance: Implement rigorous testing protocols for all communication campaigns, tracking metrics like bounce rates, opt-outs, and click-through rates to ensure message deliverability and compliance ( CAN - SPAM , TCPA ). 3. CAS , SIS Integration, Operations and Data Integrity (20%) Multi-System Integration: Oversee, audit, and optimize data integrations across the enrollment ecosystem, specifically managing data routing among CAS platforms, external vendors, Slate, and Ellucian Colleague. CAS Data Mapping: Manage the ingestion of external CAS applications, transcripts, and test scores into Slate, maintaining accurate custom source formats and mapping layouts. Ellucian Colleague Synchronization: Maintain the primary data bridge to the institutional SIS (Colleague) for prompt student ID generation, bio-demographic updates, and matriculation data processing. Process Engineering: Evaluate the enrollment funnel continuously to eliminate operational bottlenecks, reduce application turnaround times, and scale processing capabilities. Data Hygiene and Security: Supervise automated data-scrubbing routines, duplicate record management, address validation, and standard compliance rules to ensure a single, accurate source of truth in Slate and Colleague. Data Governance: Participate in ongoing institutional data governance and data platform development in collaboration with Information Technology Services and Institutional Effectiveness teams. Team Leadership: Manage, mentor, train, evaluate, and professionalize a team of operations coordinators, data specialists, and application processing staff. 4. Analytics and Reporting (15%) Executive Insights: Provide the Vice President for Enrollment Strategy with real-time data, predictive funnel tracking, and volume forecasting to inform institutional strategy using Slate and other institutional data and reporting systems. Strategic Reporting: Build, maintain, and analyze queries and visual dashboards to track recruitment KPIs, application volume, communication engagement, and conversion metrics. 5. Training and Cross-Campus Collaboration (10%) Recruiter Enablement: Create clear system documentation and lead regular training sessions for admissions staff, coaches, and faculty reviewers. Stakeholder Alignment: Act as the primary operational liaison between Enrollment Strategy, ITS , the Registrar, and Institutional Research to align practices and processes. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Required Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Advanced knowledge of enrollment operations, CRM systems, and student information systems in higher education. Advanced knowledge of Slate administration, including Configurable Joins, Rules, Reader, Deliver, portals, data imports, permissions, and workflow automation. Knowledge of data integrations, file mapping, data governance, data integrity, and information security practices. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, including the ability to build and interpret complex queries, reports, dashboards, and enrollment data. Ability to evaluate processes, identify efficiencies, and develop scalable automated workflows. Strong technical troubleshooting skills across systems, integrations, data, and business processes. Strong leadership and supervisory skills with the ability to train, mentor, and develop staff. Ability to manage multiple priorities and complex projects in a high-volume environment while maintaining accuracy and meeting deadlines. Strong written and verbal communication skills with the ability to explain technical information to technical and non-technical audiences. Ability to collaborate effectively across departments and build productive working relationships. Ability to exercise sound judgment, discretion, initiative, and attention to detail when handling sensitive information. Ability to adapt to changing technology, enrollment priorities, and institutional needs. Required Qualifications Education: Bachelor’s degree from an accredited institution. Experience: Minimum of 5 years of progressive experience in institutional data management, enrollment operations, or higher education admissions. Slate Expertise: At least 3 years of hands-on experience as a Slate Administrator (Slate Captain) with deep knowledge of Configurable Joins, Rules, Reader architecture, and Slate Deliver. Integration Experience: Proven technical experience managing automated data imports, custom file mappings, and data translations from external application platforms (e.g. CAS ). Communication Management: Demonstrated experience building multi-tiered, automated communication tracks utilizing dynamic content and variable recipient mapping. Analytical Skills: Advanced proficiency in data analysis, database management, and building complex queries. Preferred Qualifications Education: Master’s degree in data analytics, business administration, or a related field. Systems Experience: Direct experience managing complex data integrations, field mapping, and troubleshooting across Slate, CAS , and Ellucian Colleague. Influencer Marketing Tech: Experience mapping parent/influencer records to student records in Slate to execute sophisticated relational marketing strategies. Leadership: Prior experience managing professional staff and leading cross-functional software optimization projects. Distinguishing Characteristics The Director of Enrollment Operations and CRM is a strategic and technical leadership position responsible for ensuring enrollment systems, data, and operational processes effectively support the College’s recruitment and enrollment goals. The position serves as a key resource for enrollment technology and operations, with primary responsibility for Slate administration, system integrations, data integrity, workflow optimization, and operational efficiency. The Director combines technical expertise, analytical thinking, and leadership to improve processes, resolve complex system and data issues, and develop scalable solutions. This role works closely with Enrollment Strategy, Information Technology Services, the Registrar, Institutional Effectiveness, and other campus partners while leading and developing staff responsible for enrollment operations, data management, communications, and reporting. Education/Training Bachelor’s degree from an accredited institution. Experience Minimum of 5 years of progressive experience in institutional data management, enrollment operations, or higher education admissions. Licenses or Certifications Is This a Temporary Position? No Employment Conditions An offer of employment is contingent upon successful completion of the College’s background screening process. Number of Vacancies 1 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to increasing the diversity of our workforce. NCC provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process, please notify the Office of Human Resources. Determinations for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Additional Information The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the candidate’s experience, qualifications, and training. North Central College provides a comprehensive benefits package, which includes: All-inclusive medical, dental and vision group insurance Health and dependent care savings plans Employer-paid life, AD&D and disability group insurance 403(b) retirement plan with employer contributions Employee assistance plan resources Tuition waiver for full-time employees, spouse and dependent children Generous paid time off plans, including 3 weeks vacation to start 15 paid holidays per year, including 5 days during NCC Winter Break Free access to campus health center, fitness and athletic facilities, and library Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you authorized to work in the US? YES NO * Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for work authorization? YES NO Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Interest Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/15930": {
      "title": "Music Instructor Adjunct Pool",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | Music Instructor Adjunct Pool Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Music Instructor Adjunct Pool Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Posting Number F000451 Position Title Music Instructor Adjunct Pool Department Music Division Academic Affairs Faculty Status Non-Tenure Exempt Supervisor Title Chair, Department of Music Status Per Course Part-time Faculty Tenure Track No Salary Range $960 - $4,380 Position Summary The Department of Music in the College of Arts and Sciences at North Central College invites applications for an adjunct (per-course) faculty position for music. Candidates with a master’s degree or significant experience in music, instruction and education will be considered, and college-level teaching experience is preferred. North Central College values diversity, equity and inclusion and strongly encourages candidates who would enrich the diversity of the campus community to apply. Experience working with diverse student populations and communities is highly desirable. Founded in 1861, North Central College is a nationally recognized institution of about 3000 students, located in Naperville, IL, a thriving suburb just west of Chicago. The College is in a period of growth with the addition of a $60 million Science Center (2017), and a new Health Sciences and Engineering building (2020). North Central College is within walking distance of the Naperville Metra station and is next to downtown Naperville. The Naperville area is home to many excellent school districts and Naperville, IL has been ranked as one of the best places to live by Money magazine. For more information, please visit: http://www.noctrl.edu North Central College is an EEO Employer. Interested candidates should complete the application at: https://www.northcentralcollege.edu/work-here You will be asked to submit a cover letter and a curriculum vitae. For further information, please email Dr. Susan Chou, Chair, Department of Music at schou@noctrl.edu. This posting is ongoing to develop a qualified pool of candidates. If your academic area of interest, graduate degree work, and professional experience meet our programmatic needs at any time through the academic year, you may be contacted to discuss teaching opportunities. The department needs vary from semester to semester. Employment Conditions An offer of employment is contingent upon successful completion of the College’s background screening process. Number of Vacancies Multiple - Ongoing Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to increasing the diversity of our workforce. North Central College provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation, please notify the Office of Human Resource. Determinations on requests for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Additional Information The budgeted salary range for this position is $960 to $4,380. The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the selected candidate’s experience and qualifications, including equivalent years in rank, training, and field or discipline; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you authorized to work in the US? YES NO * Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for work authorization? YES NO Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter/Letter of Interest Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/15926": {
      "title": "General Maintenance/HVAC Assistant Laborer",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | General Maintenance/HVAC Assistant Laborer Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help General Maintenance/HVAC Assistant Laborer Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Posting Number S000974 Position Title General Maintenance/HVAC Assistant Laborer Department Physical Plant Administration Division Facilities Planning and Operations Supervisor Title Director of Physical Plant Status Full Time Regular Salary Range $25/Hr. Position Summary To ensure that assigned buildings, including furnishings and mechanical and electrical systems therein, are functional on a continual basis. Description of Key Responsibilities Inspect the exterior and appropriate interior areas of assigned buildings on a regular basis. Identify, prioritize and perform or report necessary maintenance activities. Promptly report safely and security concerns. Maintain regular contact with sources of work assignments (i.e., supervisor, clerk, assignment board) so that necessary activities are carried out in a timely fashion. Promptly report on completion of activities. Knowledge and ability to perform preventative maintenance, adjust, repair, and assist with the replacement of the following items: B&G pumps, hot water/steam boilers and associated controls, various hot water heaters and controls, A.C. and refrigeration units including: fan coil units, air handlers-belts, pulleys and drives, exhaust and intake fans, sump pumps, condensation pumps and lift station pumps, all type electric motors, belt/air filter changes, roof top units, various building controls, single and three phase electric, electric voltage’s ranging from 24V to 480 Volt A.C., electrical switch gear, motor starters and contractors. Maintain building access/egress areas – i.e., sidewalks, stairways – in a serviceable condition by removing debris and snow and ice. Carry out projects, which involve installations, renovations, remodeling, etc., as directed by supervisor, either individually or as part of a group. Coordinate all activities with building users and contractors (i.e., food service, cleaning services) so as to maximize efficiency and minimize disruption to building activities. May be assigned to temporarily work at other locations to substitute for personnel not available or to supplement personnel during times of unusually heavy workload. May be assigned to act in place of immediate supervisor during his/her absence, including the assumption of responsibility for that person’s duties and tasks. Perform other job-related duties and tasks as appropriately assigned. OTHER KEY RESPONSIBILITIES Support the College’s mission, vision and values, and contribute to the advancement of the College’s Strategic Plan. Demonstrate a strong commitment to continuous quality improvement. Demonstrate civil and inclusive behavior when interacting with staff, faculty, students and visitors to the College. Promote a flexible, collaborative and inclusive work and living environment and engage in educational opportunities to increase awareness and understanding of diversity and inclusion. Required Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Ability to communicate in the English language, both orally and in writing, in order to receive assignments and instructions, understand written procedures and cautions, prepare written requests, record work completed, etc. Ability to utilize basic mathematical skills necessary for calculating quantities, preparing estimates, etc. Ability to accurately interpret drawings and schematics representing electrical, mechanical, architectural and structural systems. Ability to safely and effectively transport (which may involve lifting up to 50 pounds) and utilize materials and equipment used in facility servicing and maintenance and to work safely from ladders, scaffolds, and other elevated positions. Ability to perform a variety of building maintenance tasks requiring knowledge of and skills in working with electrical and mechanical systems (including HVAC ), plumbing, carpentry, masonry and painting projects. Ability to, as necessary, work outdoors in inclement weather and to work other-than-normal and, at times, long hours. Ability to carry out work efficiently and in a manner which will minimize interference with building operations. Ability to effectively communicate with, and relate positively to, a variety of people representing various building users. Ability to learn about, and work so as to contribute positively to, the College’s organization, operation, and philosophy/culture. Distinguishing Characteristics The condition of the College’s buildings has a profound effect on the overall appearance of the campus, as well as on the daily lives of all who live, study and work there. Perhaps most striking is the impact this function has on the quality of life of students, particularly those who reside on campus. The College’s work force which is responsible for buildings and grounds maintenance is generally made up of individuals who each perform a wide variety of duties, and whose work strongly affects a diverse group of users of the facilities. Thus, employees in this area are expected to be multi-skilled, flexible, and able to work cooperatively with each other and with other members of the campus community, often without direct supervision. Because of the nature of the work, reliable and regular attendance is essential. Because individuals in this position must interact regularly with a wide variety of members of the campus community, as well as, on occasion, inspectors from City and State agencies and the College’s insurance carrier, uniforms, furnished by the College, must be worn at times determined by the position supervisor. Individuals in this position may be required to work at times that are other than, or in addition to, normalhours and days, including responding at any time to severe weather and other emergency conditions.Also, individuals in this position can expect to participate in seasonal boiler-checks and be “on-call” duringnights & weekends for specified periods (presently one week at a time, approx. every fifth week);individuals must be available by telephone during these times. Education/Training A high school diploma or equivalent is required. Experience A minimum of (1) one year of experience in building maintenance or related field is required. (specifically HVAC preferred). Licenses or Certifications A valid driver’s license and acceptable driving record are required. EPA Refrigerant Certification is required. Is This a Temporary Position? No Employment Conditions An offer of employment is contingent upon successful completion of the College’s background screening process. Number of Vacancies 1 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to increasing the diversity of our workforce. NCC provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process, please notify the Office of Human Resources. Determinations for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Additional Information The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the candidate’s experience, qualifications, and training. North Central College provides a comprehensive benefits package, which includes: All-inclusive medical, dental and vision group insurance Health and dependent care savings plans Employer-paid life, AD&D and disability group insurance 403(b) retirement plan with employer contributions Employee assistance plan resources Tuition waiver for full-time employees, spouse and dependent children Generous paid time off plans, including 3 weeks vacation to start 15 paid holidays per year, including 5 days during NCC Winter Break Free access to campus health center, fitness and athletic facilities, and library Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you authorized to work in the US? YES NO * Will you now or in the future require sponsorship for work authorization? YES NO Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Interest Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://northcentralcollege.peopleadmin.com/postings/15923": {
      "title": "Athletic Events Student Worker",
      "description": "PeopleAdmin Applicant Portal | Athletic Events Student Worker Skip to Main Content Employment Opportunities at North Central College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Athletic Events Student Worker Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Federal Work Study Eligible? Yes Posting Number ST000905 Position Title Athletic Events Student Worker Department Athletics Division Student Affairs and Athletics Supervisor Kathryn Koth Supervisor Title Athletic Events and Facilities Coordinator Description of Job Duties Assist with the setup, execution and tear-down of home athletic events including Track & Field, Soccer, Cross Country, Basketball, Lacrosse, Volleyball and Football. Assist Athletic staff with general event duties such as security, ticketing, and cleanup. Other duties as assigned. Do the job duties for this position include any driving requirements? No Job Qualifications Currently enrolled as a student at North Central College. Excellent customer service skills. Good time-management skills; must be reliable and punctual, and able to meet task deadlines. Able to lift at least 15 lbs., with or without a reasonable accommodation. Will this position be working with minors? No Approximate number of work hours per week up to 20 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement North Central College is an Equal Opportunity Employer. NCC provides reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities where appropriate. If you need reasonable accommodation for any part of the application or hiring process, please notify the Office of Human Resources at 630-637-5757 or humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu Determinations for reasonable accommodation will be made on a case-by-case basis. Open Date 04/11/2023 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you currently enrolled as a North Central College student? Yes No * Please select the answer that best describes your student work authorization: I am authorized to work for any US employer and can provide documentation, if hired. I am an international student with a US social security number authorized for campus work. I am an international student and will need to apply for a US social security number, if hired. I cannot provide US work authorization. Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Optional Documents North Central College 30 North Brainard Street, Naperville, IL 60540 630.637.5757 humanresources@hr.noctrl.edu . Back to top To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
